INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the key morphological features of an atherosclerotic plaque?','["Fibrous cap (smooth muscle cells, collagen, proteoglycans) overlying a necrotic lipid core (cholesterol crystals, foam cells, cell debris). Vulnerable plaques have a thin fibrous cap, large lipid core, and abundant macrophage infiltration at the shoulders. Calcification and neovascularisation may be present. The internal elastic lamina is attenuated beneath the plaque."]','A','Remember: FLAT-C = Fibrous cap, Lipid core, Atheroma necrosis, Thrombus risk from rupture, Calcification. Vulnerable plaque = thin cap + big lipid core + macrophages = rupture risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the "response to injury" hypothesis of atherogenesis?','["Atherosclerosis is a chronic inflammatory and healing response to endothelial injury. Key steps: (1) Endothelial injury/dysfunction from hyperlipidaemia, hypertension, smoking, toxins. (2) Lipoprotein accumulation and oxidation in the intima. (3) Monocyte adhesion (via VCAM-1, ICAM-1) and migration into intima, differentiating into macrophages. (4) Foam cell formation (macrophages ingesting ox-LDL via scavenger receptors). (5) Smooth muscle cell migration from media and proliferation. (6) Extracellular matrix synthesis forming fibrous plaque."]','A','Think of it as a wound-healing response that goes wrong: injury → inflammation → healing → plaque. The key cells are endothelium, macrophages (foam cells), T cells, and smooth muscle cells.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the major modifiable and non-modifiable risk factors for atherosclerosis?','["Non-modifiable: age (40-60 risk rises 5x), male sex (pre-menopausal women protected), family history/genetics (LDL receptor mutations). Modifiable: hyperlipidaemia (elevated LDL, low HDL), hypertension, cigarette smoking, diabetes mellitus. Additional risk factors: elevated CRP (inflammation marker), hyperhomocysteinaemia, elevated Lp(a), metabolic syndrome, physical inactivity, obesity."]','A','Mnemonic for major risk factors: HEADS → Hyperlipidaemia, Exercise deficiency (physical inactivity), Age/sex, Diabetes, Smoking. All are examinable; hypertension is sometimes added as a 5th major factor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the difference between a stable and a vulnerable (unstable) atherosclerotic plaque?','["Stable plaque: thick fibrous cap, small lipid core, few macrophages, heavy calcification, minimal inflammation. Causes stable angina by fixed stenosis. Vulnerable (unstable) plaque: thin fibrous cap (prone to rupture), large lipid core (>40% plaque volume), abundant macrophages at the shoulder regions releasing MMPs that degrade collagen, neovascularisation, intraplaque haemorrhage. Plaque rupture exposes thrombogenic core, triggering acute thrombus formation — mechanism of ACS. Note: vulnerable plaques may cause only mild stenosis (<50%) yet cause the majority of acute MIs."]','A','Counterintuitive fact: the plaques that cause ACS are often NOT the most obstructive ones. A 40% stenosis with a thin cap is more dangerous than a 70% stenosis with a thick stable cap. Statins stabilise plaques by reducing inflammation and MMP activity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','flashcard','Describe the progression from fatty streak to complicated plaque in atherosclerosis.','["1. Fatty streak: earliest visible lesion; yellow intimal discolouration; lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells) and T lymphocytes in intima; present in children; reversible. 2. Fibrous (atherosclerotic) plaque: fibrous cap of SMCs and collagen overlying necrotic lipid core; yellow-white raised intimal lesion; causes luminal narrowing. 3. Complicated plaque: calcification, ulceration, intraplaque haemorrhage (from rupture of neovessels), superimposed thrombus. Complications: occlusion, embolisation, aneurysm formation from medial destruction."]','A','Progression is not inevitable — fatty streaks can regress, especially with risk factor control. The key transition is from stable fibrous plaque to complicated plaque with rupture.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','flashcard','How do foam cells form and why does this process not self-regulate?','["Oxidised LDL (ox-LDL) is taken up by macrophages via scavenger receptors (SR-A, CD36). Unlike LDL receptor-mediated uptake, scavenger receptor expression is NOT downregulated by intracellular cholesterol accumulation. This means macrophages continue to ingest ox-LDL uncontrollably, becoming lipid-laden foam cells. Foam cells also recruit more monocytes (via MCP-1, M-CSF), amplifying the process. Dead foam cells release their lipid into the extracellular space, forming the necrotic core."]','A','Key distinction: LDL receptor = regulated (downregulated when cell is cholesterol-replete). Scavenger receptor = unregulated (keeps ingesting ox-LDL regardless). This unregulated uptake is why dyslipidaemia causes unstoppable foam cell accumulation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','hard','flashcard','What is the role of nitric oxide (NO) in protecting against atherosclerosis, and why does endothelial dysfunction reduce NO bioavailability?','["NO (produced by eNOS in endothelial cells from L-arginine) protects against atherosclerosis by: (1) Vasodilation (opposes endothelin/AngII vasoconstriction); (2) Inhibiting platelet aggregation and adhesion; (3) Inhibiting monocyte adhesion (suppressing VCAM-1/ICAM-1 expression); (4) Reducing smooth muscle proliferation. Endothelial dysfunction reduces NO via: oxidative stress (ox-LDL, cigarette smoke → superoxide destroys NO forming peroxynitrite), reduced eNOS activity, reduced BH4 cofactor. This shifts the endothelium toward a pro-inflammatory, prothrombotic, vasoconstrictive state."]','A','NO = the endothelium''s master anti-atherosclerotic molecule. Loss of NO bioavailability is central to endothelial dysfunction and atherogenesis. Statins, ACE inhibitors, and exercise all increase eNOS activity — one mechanism of their cardiovascular benefit.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','hard','flashcard','What is the role of matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) in plaque instability?','["MMPs (collagenases MMP-1, MMP-8; gelatinases MMP-2, MMP-9; stromelysins MMP-3; macrophage elastase MMP-12) are secreted by macrophages, SMCs, and endothelial cells within plaques. They degrade collagen and elastin in the fibrous cap. Their activity is counterbalanced by TIMPs (tissue inhibitors of metalloproteinases). In vulnerable plaques, the balance tips toward MMP activity: (1) Abundant macrophages secrete more MMPs; (2) Inflammation reduces SMC numbers (apoptosis), reducing TIMP and collagen synthesis; (3) Net result: thin fibrous cap vulnerable to mechanical stress and rupture."]','A','Remember: MMP = Matrix Munching Proteins — macrophages use them to digest the fibrous cap, making the plaque unstable. Statins reduce plaque inflammation and MMP activity, stabilising plaques — benefit beyond cholesterol lowering (pleiotropic effects).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the major consequences of atherosclerotic disease in different vascular beds?','["Coronary arteries: stable angina (fixed stenosis >70%), ACS/MI (plaque rupture + thrombus), sudden cardiac death, chronic IHD. Carotid/cerebral arteries: TIA, ischaemic stroke (from embolism or in situ thrombosis), lacunar infarcts. Aorta: abdominal aortic aneurysm (medial weakening), atheroembolism (cholesterol emboli). Renal arteries: renovascular hypertension, ischaemic nephropathy. Peripheral arteries (iliofemoral): intermittent claudication, rest pain, critical limb ischaemia, gangrene. Mesenteric arteries: intestinal angina, ischaemic colitis."]','A','Atherosclerosis is a systemic disease — if present in one bed, suspect it in others. Ankle-brachial index (ABI) screens for peripheral artery disease; carotid US for stroke risk; coronary CT for subclinical CAD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','easy','flashcard','What is arteriosclerosis and how does it differ from atherosclerosis?','["Arteriosclerosis is a general term for hardening/thickening of arterial walls with loss of elasticity, encompassing three distinct patterns: (1) Atherosclerosis — intimal plaques in large/medium arteries; the most clinically important form. (2) Mönckeberg medial calcific sclerosis — calcification of the media of muscular arteries without intimal involvement or luminal narrowing; does not cause ischaemia; seen in elderly and diabetics. (3) Arteriolosclerosis — affects small arteries/arterioles; two subtypes: hyaline (benign hypertension/diabetes) and hyperplastic (malignant hypertension)."]','A','Key distinction: Mönckeberg sclerosis calcifies the MEDIA (pipestem arteries) but does NOT narrow the lumen — it is clinically benign but gives false-high ankle-brachial index. Atherosclerosis affects the INTIMA and NARROWS the lumen — clinically dangerous.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','easy','flashcard','What are the four clinical syndromes of ischaemic heart disease (IHD)?','["1. Angina pectoris: chest pain from ischaemia without infarction. Types: (a) Stable angina — predictable, exertional, relieved by rest/nitrates; fixed stenosis >70%. (b) Unstable angina — rest pain, crescendo, plaque rupture with non-occlusive thrombus. (c) Variant (Prinzmetal) angina — rest pain from coronary vasospasm. 2. Myocardial infarction (MI): necrosis from sustained ischaemia; STEMI (complete occlusion) vs NSTEMI (incomplete occlusion). 3. Chronic IHD (ischaemic cardiomyopathy): progressive HF from cumulative ischaemic damage. 4. Sudden cardiac death: VF from ischaemia-induced arrhythmia."]','A','In >90% of IHD, the underlying cause is obstructive coronary atherosclerosis. The clinical syndrome depends on whether the ischaemia is: transient (angina), sustained partial (NSTEMI), sustained complete (STEMI), or arrhythmogenic (SCD).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','easy','flashcard','Describe the sequence of histological changes in acute myocardial infarction over time.','["0-4 hours: No light microscopy changes (earliest changes on EM — mitochondrial swelling). Wavy fibre change at 1-4 hours. 4-12 hours: Coagulation necrosis begins — hypereosinophilic cytoplasm, pyknotic nuclei. 12-24 hours: Neutrophilic infiltration begins; loss of nuclei. 1-3 days: Peak neutrophilic infiltration; myocyte ghosts. 3-7 days: Macrophage infiltration, phagocytosis of debris, yellow-white softening (maximal risk of rupture). 1-3 weeks: Granulation tissue (ingrowth of vessels and fibroblasts from margins). 6+ weeks: Dense fibrous (collagen) scar — white and contracted."]','A','Memory timeline: 0-4h = invisible; 4-12h = eosinophilia + waviness; 1-3d = neutrophils; 3-7d = macrophages (RUPTURE RISK); 1-3wk = granulation; 6wk+ = scar. Rupture peaks at 3-7 days when the wall is softest.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','flashcard','What are the complications of acute myocardial infarction and their typical time course?','["Immediate (minutes-hours): Arrhythmias (VF — most common cause of early death); cardiogenic shock (loss of >40% LV mass). Hours-days: Pericarditis (fibrinous, over the infarct at 1-3 days). Days (3-7): Rupture of free wall (haemopericardium/tamponade), papillary muscle (acute MR), or ventricular septum (acquired VSD) — all at peak of myocardial softening. Days-weeks: Mural thrombus (over dyskinetic myocardium, source of emboli); infarct expansion/thinning. Weeks-months: Ventricular aneurysm (from scar expansion); Dressler syndrome (2-10 weeks, autoimmune pericarditis); chronic IHD/HF from scarring."]','A','Temporal hook for complications: ''3R'' = Rupture at 3-7 days (free wall, papillary muscle, septum); Dressler at ''3 Weeks+'' (post-immune). Mural thrombus = L→R emboli risk whenever there is dyskinesia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','flashcard','Which coronary artery supplies which territory and what is the pattern of infarction with each occlusion?','["LAD (left anterior descending): supplies anterior LV wall, anterior 2/3 of interventricular septum, apex. Occlusion → anterior MI (leads V1-V4); most common, largest infarcts, highest mortality. Most common site for post-MI aneurysm and mural thrombus. RCA (right coronary artery): supplies inferior LV wall (in right-dominant systems), posterior septum, SA and AV nodes. Occlusion → inferior MI (II, III, aVF); RV infarction; AV block; sinus node dysfunction. LCx (left circumflex): supplies lateral LV wall. Occlusion → lateral MI (I, aVL, V5-V6). Right dominance (70%): RCA gives off posterior descending artery. Left dominance (15%): LCx gives off PDA."]','A','LAD = the ''widow maker'' — largest territory, worst prognosis if occluded proximally. RCA → think inferior MI + blocks (SA/AV nodes affected).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','flashcard','What is the wavefront phenomenon of myocardial necrosis?','["Described by Reimer and Jennings: after complete coronary occlusion, myocardial necrosis begins in the subendocardium (most vulnerable zone — farthest from coronary supply, highest oxygen demand, highest wall stress) and progresses as a ''wavefront'' toward the epicardium over 3-6 hours. Subendocardial infarction (NSTEMI) occurs with partial or transient occlusion. Transmural infarction (STEMI) occurs with sustained complete occlusion >6 hours. Reperfusion within 20-40 minutes → minimal injury; within 3-6 hours → significant salvage. After 6 hours → little benefit."]','A','The wavefront concept explains: (1) Why NSTEMI = subendocardial; STEMI = transmural. (2) Why early reperfusion (thrombolytics/PCI) saves myocardium — the wavefront has not yet reached the epicardium.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','hard','flashcard','What is reperfusion injury and what are its mechanisms?','["Paradoxically, restoring blood flow to ischaemic myocardium causes additional injury: (1) Calcium overload: ischaemia impairs Ca2+ pumps; reperfusion restores ionic gradients but allows a burst of Ca2+ influx → hypercontracture and mitochondrial permeability transition pore (mPTP) opening. (2) Oxidative stress: reoxygenation generates reactive oxygen species (ROS) via xanthine oxidase and mitochondria, damaging membranes/proteins. (3) Inflammatory activation: reperfusion activates complement, neutrophil adhesion (via P-selectin upregulation), and cytokine release → microvascular plugging and further injury. (4) mPTP opening → cytochrome c release → apoptosis."]','A','Reperfusion injury = the price of saving the heart. mPTP is the key effector. This is why MRI shows ''no-reflow'' zones even after successful PCI — microvascular obstruction from reperfusion injury. Cyclosporine (blocks mPTP) was studied but not yet proven clinically.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','hard','flashcard','What are the pathological mechanisms of sudden cardiac death (SCD)?','["SCD is defined as unexpected death within 1 hour of symptom onset. Mechanism: lethal arrhythmia (ventricular fibrillation >80%, asystole). Underlying substrate (90% of cases): severe obstructive coronary artery disease (often multi-vessel). Key: 80-90% of resuscitated SCD survivors show NO evidence of MI (no enzyme rise, no ECG changes) — the arrhythmia arises from ischaemic but viable myocardium. Other causes include HCM (leading cause in young athletes <35), arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, long QT syndrome (channelopathy), Brugada syndrome, myocarditis, cardiac sarcoid, cocaine/stimulant abuse. Triggers include acute ischaemia, catecholamine surges (emotion, exercise), electrolyte disturbances."]','A','Key SCD fact: NO MI in most cases. SCD = electrical death, not plumbing death. The ICD (implantable cardioverter-defibrillator) is the only intervention proven to reduce SCD mortality in high-risk patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','easy','flashcard','What cardiac biomarkers are used to diagnose MI and what are their time courses?','["Troponin I and T (cardiac-specific): Rise 3-6 hours post-MI, peak at 24-48 hours, remain elevated 5-14 days. Most sensitive and specific for myocardial necrosis. High-sensitivity troponin (hsTnI/T) detects necrosis earlier. CK-MB (creatine kinase MB isoform): Rise 4-8 hours, peak 24 hours, normalises in 48-72 hours — useful for detecting reinfarction (resetting of levels). Myoglobin: Earliest rise (1-4 hours) but non-cardiac-specific. LDH (lactate dehydrogenase, specifically LDH1 > LDH2): Rises late (24-48 hours), peaks 3-6 days, persists 10-14 days — historically used to diagnose late-presenting MI."]','A','Troponin = best single test; hsTnI can rule in/out MI within 1-3 hours with serial measurements. CK-MB = useful for reinfarction. ''Flipped LDH'' (LDH1 > LDH2) is a classic exam finding for late MI diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','flashcard','What gross changes are visible in the infarcted myocardium at different time points post-MI?','["First 12 hours: No gross changes (TTC stain shows pale/white area from dehydrogenase loss). 12-24 hours: Pale, mottled area. 1-3 days: More obvious pallor; softening begins. 3-7 days: Yellow-white, soft, friable centre (maximal softening — rupture risk peak); hyperaemic border from inflammatory reaction. 1-3 weeks: Red-pink vascularised granulation tissue at margins gradually replacing necrotic centre. 6+ weeks: Grey-white, firm, contracted fibrous scar (depressed below surrounding myocardium)."]','A','The progression: pale → pale yellow → soft yellow → pink-red granulation → white scar. Think ''bruise healing'': pallor, yellow softening, red granulation, white scar. A ''firm white scar'' = healed MI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','flashcard','What is hibernating myocardium and how does it differ from stunned myocardium?','["Hibernating myocardium: Myocardium that is chronically hypocontractile due to chronic low-grade ischaemia (reduced resting perfusion) but remains viable (metabolically active). It ''adapts'' to reduced supply by reducing its work. Function recovers with revascularisation. Clinically: patients with severe CAD and significant LV dysfunction may have substantial hibernating myocardium recoverable with PCI/CABG. Stunned myocardium: Reversible post-ischaemic contractile dysfunction after a brief ischaemic episode followed by reperfusion. Normal blood flow is restored but function is temporarily impaired (hours to days). Recovers spontaneously without further treatment."]','A','Hibernating = sleeping with reduced blood flow (needs revascularisation to wake up). Stunned = knocked out after reperfusion (wakes up on its own). Both are VIABLE myocardium — both can recover function.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','easy','flashcard','What is the Frank-Starling mechanism and how does it compensate in heart failure?','["The Frank-Starling law states that within physiological limits, increased ventricular end-diastolic volume (preload/stretch) increases sarcomere length and force of contraction, thereby increasing stroke volume. In early heart failure: the failing ventricle dilates (increases preload) to maintain cardiac output via the Starling mechanism. Limitation: as the ventricle dilates beyond the optimal sarcomere length (~2.2 μm), additional stretch no longer increases force — the Starling curve plateau is reached. Beyond this, further dilation increases wall stress (Laplace) without increasing output — maladaptive. Clinical sign: raised JVP and peripheral oedema reflect the need for high filling pressures to maintain output."]','A','Starling = the heart''s short-term emergency mechanism. The problem: heart failure shifts the Starling curve rightward AND flattens it — the heart needs more preload to achieve less output.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','easy','flashcard','What is the difference between systolic and diastolic heart failure?','["Systolic HF (HFrEF — reduced ejection fraction <40%): Impaired ventricular contractility. Ventricle dilates (eccentric hypertrophy). Low EF. Causes: ischaemic cardiomyopathy, DCM, myocarditis. Treated with ACEi/ARB, beta-blockers, MRAs, SGLT2i, diuretics. Diastolic HF (HFpEF — preserved ejection fraction ≥50%): Normal or near-normal contractility and EF. Impaired relaxation and increased chamber stiffness → elevated filling pressures → pulmonary congestion and reduced cardiac output at rest or with exercise. Causes: hypertensive LVH, HCM, restrictive cardiomyopathy. Elderly women with hypertension = classic. Treatment: less evidence-based; diuretics for congestion, control BP and rate."]','A','HFrEF = pump failure (weak heart). HFpEF = stiff heart (fails to fill, not to pump). Both present with dyspnoea and elevated BNP but management differs significantly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','flashcard','What are the neurohormonal compensatory mechanisms activated in heart failure and why are they ultimately maladaptive?','["1. Sympathetic NS activation: Increases HR and contractility (short-term compensation). Long-term: beta-1 receptor downregulation, myocyte apoptosis, arrhythmias, increased afterload. 2. RAAS activation: Angiotensin II → vasoconstriction, aldosterone secretion (Na+/H2O retention, K+ loss), and direct myocardial fibrosis; worsens volume/pressure overload. 3. ADH (vasopressin) release: Water retention → dilutional hyponatraemia. 4. Endothelin release: Potent vasoconstriction. 5. BNP/ANP (counter-regulatory): Natriuresis and vasodilation — salutary but overwhelmed. All neurohormonal systems initially maintain perfusion pressure but ultimately cause progressive ventricular remodelling (hypertrophy, fibrosis, apoptosis), worsening HF over time."]','A','The RAAS and SNS = the double-edged sword of HF compensation. ACE inhibitors, ARBs, beta-blockers, aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone), and ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) all target these maladaptive neurohormonal pathways.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','flashcard','What is cardiac remodelling in heart failure and what molecular changes occur?','["Cardiac remodelling: progressive changes in ventricular size, shape, and function after myocardial injury. Involves: (1) Cardiomyocyte hypertrophy — increased cell size (not number); (2) Foetal gene re-expression — beta-MHC (lower ATPase, less efficient) replaces alpha-MHC; ANF, BNP expression increases; (3) Sarcomere dysfunction — decreased SERCA2a activity (impaired Ca2+ reuptake → impaired relaxation and excess Ca2+ in systole); (4) Calcium handling abnormalities — leaky RyR2 (ryanodine receptor) → arrhythmias; (5) Interstitial fibrosis from myofibroblast activation (AngII, TGF-β); (6) Myocyte apoptosis from oxidative stress and cytokines; (7) Beta-1 receptor downregulation → reduced inotropic reserve."]','A','The foetal gene programme is a key concept: beta-MHC and ANF/BNP re-expression in pathological hypertrophy are markers of maladaptive remodelling. Physiological hypertrophy (exercise) does NOT activate foetal genes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','flashcard','What are the clinical features of left vs right heart failure?','["Left heart failure: Dyspnoea (exertional then rest), orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, pulmonary oedema, cardiac asthma (bronchospasm from oedema). Pathology: pulmonary venous hypertension → transudative pulmonary oedema → haemosiderin-laden macrophages (''heart failure cells'') in alveoli; brown induration of lungs. Right heart failure (often secondary to LHF): Peripheral pitting oedema, raised JVP, hepatomegaly (pulsatile), ascites, pleural effusions (bilateral), anasarca. Pathology: systemic venous congestion → ''nutmeg liver'' (centrilobular congestion/necrosis) → cardiac cirrhosis in severe chronic cases; congestive splenomegaly."]','A','LHF → goes backward to lungs (pulmonary oedema). RHF → goes backward to body (peripheral oedema, JVP, hepatomegaly). Most RHF is caused by LHF (pulmonary hypertension from chronically elevated LA pressure).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','hard','flashcard','Describe pathological vs physiological cardiac hypertrophy at the molecular level.','["Physiological hypertrophy (exercise, pregnancy): Concentric or eccentric depending on type of exercise; foetal gene programme NOT activated; cardiomyocyte dimensions increase but ratio maintained; normal SERCA2a; no fibrosis; reversible. Pathological hypertrophy (pressure/volume overload, neurohormonal activation, ischaemia): Foetal gene re-expression (beta-MHC, ANF); decreased SERCA2a → diastolic dysfunction; interstitial fibrosis; myocyte apoptosis; chamber dilation; decreased contractile reserve. Key signalling pathways: Pathological — calcineurin/NFAT, PKC; Physiological — PI3K/Akt/IGF-1. These pathways represent potential therapeutic targets to convert pathological hypertrophy back to physiological or to prevent its development."]','A','The crucial distinction: physiological hypertrophy = adaptive and reversible; pathological = maladaptive and progressive. This is why elite athletes have big hearts that work well, whereas hypertensive patients have hypertrophied hearts that fail.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','hard','flashcard','What are the haemodynamic consequences of acute vs chronic heart failure and how do compensatory mechanisms differ?','["Acute decompensated HF: Sudden reduction in cardiac output activates emergency responses — immediate sympathetic surge (increases HR, contractility), rapid renal vasoconstriction (reducing urine output), peripheral vasoconstriction (maintaining BP). No time for structural adaptation. Result: acute pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock. Chronic HF: Time allows structural adaptations — cardiac hypertrophy, ventricular dilation (Starling), increased circulating blood volume (RAAS/ADH → Na+/H2O retention). These allow patients to be haemodynamically compensated at rest (euvolaemic, normal resting BP) despite significantly impaired pump function. Decompensation occurs when these compensatory mechanisms are overwhelmed (infection, arrhythmia, excessive dietary Na+)."]','A','Acute HF = emergency compensatory reflexes. Chronic HF = structural adaptation that temporarily masks the problem. Sudden decompensation = compensatory mechanisms overwhelmed, usually precipitated by a trigger.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','easy','flashcard','What is high-output heart failure and what are its causes?','["High-output heart failure occurs when the heart cannot meet the excessive demands of a state requiring abnormally elevated cardiac output, even though cardiac function may be initially normal. Causes (all cause low peripheral vascular resistance or AV shunting): Severe anaemia (reduced O2 delivery → vasodilation), thyrotoxicosis (thyroid hormone → increased heart rate and O2 demand), Paget disease of bone (high vascularity of pagetoid bone → AV shunting), beriberi (thiamine deficiency → vasodilation), arteriovenous fistulae, pregnancy (high-flow state). In chronic cases, the heart eventually fails despite initially elevated output."]','A','High-output HF = the heart is working hard but cannot keep up with the body''s demands. Classic mnemonic for causes: A THIEF = Anaemia, Thyrotoxicosis, High-flow states (Paget, AV fistula), Infections (sepsis), Expanded blood volume (pregnancy), beriberi (B1 deficiency).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the two types of hypertensive heart disease and what are the diagnostic criteria for systemic HHD?','["Systemic (left-sided) HHD: Results from chronic systemic hypertension causing LV pressure overload. Diagnostic criteria (both required): (1) Left ventricular hypertrophy (usually concentric) without other cardiovascular pathology to explain it (no aortic stenosis, no HCM); (2) Clinical or pathological evidence of hypertension (history, renal arteriosclerosis, hypertensive retinopathy). LV weight >500g in men, >400g in women suggests significant hypertrophy. Pulmonary (right-sided) HHD (Cor pulmonale): Results from pulmonary hypertension from lung disease (COPD, PE, pulmonary fibrosis) causing RV pressure overload and RV hypertrophy/dilation."]','A','Systemic HHD = hypertension → LVH. Cor pulmonale = lung disease → pulmonary HTN → RVH. Key: LVH in HHD is CONCENTRIC (pressure overload adds sarcomeres in parallel). Must exclude other causes of LVH (aortic stenosis, HCM).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the two patterns of small vessel disease caused by hypertension?','["1. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis: Seen in benign (chronic) hypertension and diabetes. Homogeneous, glassy, pink (eosinophilic) thickening of arteriolar walls from plasma protein insudation (immunoglobulins, complement) through damaged endothelium into the vessel wall, combined with increased basement membrane matrix. Narrows the lumen; increases peripheral vascular resistance. Reversible component: vascular smooth muscle hypertrophy. 2. Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis (onion-skin lesion): Seen in malignant hypertension. Concentric laminated thickening of the arteriolar wall from proliferating smooth muscle cells and reduplicated basement membrane — ''onion-skin'' pattern. Often accompanied by fibrinoid necrosis. Causes acute end-organ damage (acute renal failure, hypertensive encephalopathy)."]','A','Hyaline = benign hypertension; pale pink glass. Hyperplastic (onion-skin) + fibrinoid necrosis = MALIGNANT hypertension. Both narrow arterioles but malignant HTN causes acute end-organ damage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','How does chronic hypertension lead to heart failure via LVH and what is the sequence of events?','["Sequence: Chronic hypertension → increased afterload → LV must work harder to eject against high aortic pressure → concentric LVH (sarcomeres added in parallel, wall thickness increases, cavity size maintained or reduced). Initially adaptive: normalises wall stress (Laplace: stress = P×r/2h; increased h compensates for increased P). Maladaptive consequences: (1) Increased myocardial oxygen demand from increased mass; (2) Subendocardial ischaemia from microvascular disease; (3) Impaired diastolic relaxation (stiff hypertrophied wall) → HFpEF; (4) Interstitial fibrosis → arrhythmias (SCD risk); (5) Eventually systolic dysfunction (decompensation) → dilated cardiomyopathy pattern."]','A','The natural history of untreated hypertensive heart disease: Hypertension → Concentric LVH (compensated) → Diastolic HF (HFpEF) → Decompensated systolic HF (HFrEF). Each stage can last years; early treatment (antihypertensives) can reverse LVH.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','What is cystic medial necrosis and in which conditions does it occur?','["Cystic medial necrosis (medionecrosis): Pathological change in the aortic media characterised by (1) accumulation of mucoid (myxoid) extracellular matrix in pools between smooth muscle cells, (2) loss of elastic fibres (fragmentation), (3) smooth muscle cell death/loss. These ''cysts'' of mucoid material are the hallmark. It weakens the aortic media, predisposing to aortic dilation and dissection. Conditions: (1) Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1 mutation → abnormal elastin microfibrils); (2) Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (collagen mutations); (3) Chronic hypertension (haemodynamic stress accelerates medial degeneration); (4) Idiopathic in elderly; (5) Associated with bicuspid aortic valve and aortic root dilation."]','A','Cystic medial necrosis = the histological substrate for aortic dissection. Marfan = classic teaching case; hypertension = most common real-world cause of dissection. The mucoid pools are not true cysts — they are spaces filled with glycosaminoglycans.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','What is cor pulmonale and how does chronic lung disease cause it?','["Cor pulmonale = right ventricular hypertrophy and/or dilation from pulmonary hypertension caused by lung disease (excludes LHF as the cause). Pathogenesis: Lung disease (COPD, pulmonary fibrosis, recurrent PE, OSA) → (1) Alveolar hypoxia → hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV) — acute and reversible; (2) Structural pulmonary vascular changes — medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis of pulmonary arteries from chronic hypoxia and inflammation; (3) Loss of pulmonary vascular bed (emphysema destroys capillaries); (4) All increase pulmonary vascular resistance → pulmonary hypertension → RV pressure overload → RV hypertrophy → eventual RV failure. Acute cor pulmonale (massive PE): sudden RV dilation without hypertrophy."]','A','HPV = short-term response (reversible vasospasm). Structural vascular remodelling = long-term irreversible change. Once structural changes occur, pulmonary HTN becomes fixed and cor pulmonale follows.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','hard','flashcard','What is the Laplace relationship and how does it explain both the compensatory and ultimately maladaptive nature of cardiac hypertrophy?','["Laplace law: Wall stress (σ) = (P × r) / (2 × h), where P = intracavitary pressure, r = chamber radius, h = wall thickness. In hypertension (increased P): wall stress rises. Compensatory response — hypertrophy increases h, normalising wall stress (σ stays normal despite high P). Adaptive phase: normal wall stress preserved. Maladaptive when: (1) Hypertrophy increases oxygen demand (more mass to perfuse); (2) Subendocardial ischaemia from microvascular disease; (3) If the ventricle begins to dilate (increased r), even with increased h, the ratio P×r/2h rises → increased wall stress → further hypertrophy and dilation (vicious cycle of decompensation). Volume overload (increased r) → eccentric hypertrophy with cavity dilation."]','A','Laplace is the mathematical backbone of cardiac compensation. Increased wall stress = increased oxygen demand = ischaemia = further dysfunction. The only way out is to increase h (thickness) — which works until the ventricle dilates (increased r overwhelms increased h).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','hard','flashcard','How does malignant hypertension differ from benign hypertension in terms of vascular pathology and end-organ damage?','["Benign (chronic) hypertension: Gradual onset; HTN >140/90 mmHg; vascular lesion = hyaline arteriolosclerosis (plasma protein insudation, homogeneous pink thickening); causes chronic ischaemia of end organs over years → nephrosclerosis (small contracted kidneys with granular surfaces), retinal artery narrowing, LVH. Malignant hypertension: Sudden severe HTN typically >200/120 mmHg; vascular lesion = hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis (onion-skin SMC proliferation) + FIBRINOID NECROSIS of arteriolar walls (the key distinguishing feature — plasma protein coagulation in vessel wall indicates acute injury); acute end-organ damage: acute renal failure (oliguric), hypertensive encephalopathy, papilloedema, retinal flame haemorrhages/exudates."]','A','Fibrinoid necrosis = the hallmark of MALIGNANT hypertension. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis = benign hypertension. Fibrinoid = pink, smudgy, acellular material in the vessel wall (like fuchsinophilic coagulated protein). This causes acute kidney injury and neurological emergency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the renal manifestations of chronic hypertension and how does hypertension-related nephropathy progress?','["Benign nephrosclerosis: Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent arterioles → reduced glomerular perfusion → focal glomerulosclerosis → tubular atrophy → interstitial fibrosis. Grossly: bilaterally small, symmetrically granular kidneys (''granular contracted kidneys'') — fine granularity from alternating areas of focal glomerulosclerosis (scars) and compensatory hypertrophy. Clinically: mild proteinuria and slowly progressive CKD. Malignant nephrosclerosis: Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis + fibrinoid necrosis of afferent arterioles → acute ischaemic glomerular injury → rapidly progressive renal failure requiring dialysis. ''Flea-bitten kidney'': petechial haemorrhages on renal surface from rupture of glomerular capillaries."]','A','Benign nephrosclerosis = granular contracted kidney (slowly shrinking). Malignant nephrosclerosis = ''flea-bitten kidney'' (petechiae) = acute renal emergency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','easy','flashcard','What is calcific aortic stenosis and how does it differ in normal vs bicuspid valves?','["Calcific aortic stenosis (CAS) is the most common valvular abnormality. Two patterns: (1) Senile calcific aortic stenosis (anatomically normal tricuspid valve): Calcium deposits at the hinge points/base of leaflets from ''wear and tear'' — similar process to atherosclerosis (hyperlipidaemia, hypertension, inflammation). Leaflet tips remain free; no commissural fusion (distinguishes from rheumatic). Presents in 7th-9th decade. (2) Calcific stenosis of bicuspid aortic valve: Same process but eccentric turbulent flow accelerates calcification. Presents 1-2 decades earlier (5th-6th decade). Bicuspid AV = 1-2% of population; M>F; associated with aortic root dilation, dissection, coarctation."]','A','No commissural fusion in calcific AS = distinguishes from rheumatic AS. Calcium builds up at the BASE of leaflets, not the tips — leaflet tips are still relatively free. Bicuspid valve = same disease, earlier age.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','easy','flashcard','What are the morphological features of rheumatic valvular disease?','["Acute phase: Fibrinous vegetations (verrucae) — small (1-2 mm), firm, bead-like, along the LINE OF CLOSURE of valve leaflets (usually on the atrial surface of the AV valves and ventricular surface of the semilunar valves). Aschoff bodies in the myocardium. Chronic/healed phase: Leaflet thickening and fibrosis, commissural fusion (specific to rheumatic disease — distinguishes from calcific AS), chordal thickening/fusion and shortening. Mitral valve most commonly affected, then aortic, tricuspid (rarely pulmonary). Net effect: mitral stenosis (''fish-mouth'' or ''buttonhole'' deformity), or combined stenosis and regurgitation. LA dilation from MS → atrial fibrillation, left atrial thrombus, pulmonary hypertension."]','A','Commissural fusion = pathognomonic of rheumatic disease. ''Fish-mouth'' appearance of rheumatic MS on echocardiography. The acute vegetations are on the LINE OF CLOSURE (highest mechanical stress area where antibody deposition occurs).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','flashcard','What is mitral valve prolapse (myxomatous degeneration) and what are its pathological features?','["Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation in developed countries (~2-3% of population; women > men). Myxomatous degeneration: abnormal accumulation of myxoid (mucoid) extracellular matrix (proteoglycans) in the spongiosa layer of leaflets, causing: (1) Leaflet enlargement and billowing; (2) Elongated, thinned, or ruptured chordae tendineae; (3) Annular dilation. Gross: ballooning/billowing posterior leaflet (most common) into LA during systole. Auscultation: mid-systolic click followed by late systolic murmur. Associations: Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos. Most asymptomatic; complications (<5%): severe MR, atrial fibrillation, infective endocarditis, SCD (rare), paradoxical embolism."]','A','MVP = most common cause of MR in developed world. Myxomatous = too much proteoglycan in the spongiosa layer → floppy leaflets. Mid-systolic click from sudden tensing of chordae when leaflets billow into LA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','flashcard','What are the different forms of endocarditis and how do they differ pathologically?','["1. Infective endocarditis (IE): Microorganisms colonise valves (particularly previously damaged or abnormal valves). Acute IE (S. aureus): large, destructive, friable vegetations that rapidly destroy leaflets. Subacute IE (Streptococcus viridans): smaller, less destructive vegetations on abnormal valves. IV drug users: right-sided (tricuspid) + S. aureus. Consequences: septic emboli, regurgitation, heart block, mycotic aneurysms. 2. Non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis (NBTE/marantic): small, sterile, bland fibrin-platelet vegetations on normal valves; associated with debilitating illness, malignancy, hypercoagulable states; embolise causing strokes. 3. Libman-Sacks (SLE): Small vegetations on BOTH surfaces of any valve; associated with antiphospholipid antibodies. 4. Rheumatic: Beaded vegetations along line of closure."]','A','Key comparison: IE = large, destructive, infected; NBTE = small, sterile, bland, embolic; Libman-Sacks = BOTH valve surfaces (unique), SLE; Rheumatic = line of closure, heals with commissural fusion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','flashcard','How does chronic mitral regurgitation differ from acute mitral regurgitation in terms of cardiac adaptation?','["Chronic MR: The LA and LV have time to dilate gradually (eccentric hypertrophy — sarcomeres added in series) to accommodate the regurgitant volume. LA enlarges (chronically elevated LA pressure → atrial fibrillation). LV dilates (volume overload) — large compliant LA accommodates regurgitant volume at lower pressure, so pulmonary oedema develops late. Patient may remain compensated for years. Acute MR (e.g., from papillary muscle rupture post-MI or chordal rupture): Normal-sized, non-compliant LA receives sudden large regurgitant volume → massive acute rise in LA pressure → acute pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock. Heart sounds: loud pansystolic murmur in chronic MR; acute MR may have soft or no murmur (low pressure difference across valve) despite severe regurgitation."]','A','Acute MR = catastrophic (no time for adaptation). Chronic MR = gradual compensation, years of tolerance. This is the ''compliance principle'': a chronically dilated LA accommodates large volumes at low pressures.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','hard','flashcard','What is the pathogenesis of rheumatic fever and how does molecular mimicry lead to cardiac valve damage?','["Group A beta-haemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes) pharyngitis triggers an immune response. Cross-reactive antibodies and T cells target streptococcal M protein (and other antigens) that share epitopes with cardiac tissue antigens (including myosin, tropomyosin, laminin, and glycoproteins in valvular tissue). This molecular mimicry causes: (1) Acute carditis: pancarditis — pericarditis (fibrinous), myocarditis (Aschoff bodies), endocarditis (fibrinous vegetations on valves). (2) Repeated episodes damage valves progressively (each streptococcal infection → new immune attack). (3) Healing → scarring, commissural fusion, chordal thickening → chronic rheumatic valvular disease. Prevention: penicillin prophylaxis eliminates streptococcal carriage and prevents recurrent attacks."]','A','Molecular mimicry = the immune system mistakes ''self'' for ''strep''. Key: REPEATED attacks cause cumulative valve damage. Penicillin prophylaxis (monthly benzathine penicillin G) prevents recurrent strep infections and thus prevents progressive valve damage in endemic areas.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','hard','flashcard','What are the haemodynamic consequences of aortic stenosis and why do the classic symptoms occur in a specific sequence?','["Severe AS (valve area <1 cm²; mean gradient >40 mmHg): LV must generate very high pressure to open the aortic valve → severe LV pressure overload → concentric LVH (compensatory). Classic symptom triad (SAD) appears in order: (1) Angina: LVH increases oxygen demand; LVH compresses intramural coronary vessels; coronary reserve is reduced even without epicardial CAD; exertional angina occurs. Prognosis: survival ~5 years after onset. (2) Syncope: Fixed cardiac output (cannot increase with exercise because stenotic valve limits flow); exertional peripheral vasodilation → BP drops → cerebral hypoperfusion. Prognosis: ~3 years. (3) Dyspnoea (HF): LV finally fails → rising LVEDP → pulmonary hypertension. Prognosis: ~2 years. Valve replacement is indicated when symptoms begin."]','A','SAD = Syncope, Angina, Dyspnoea (in order of worsening prognosis). Mnemonic for prognosis: ''SADly, 5-3-2 years''. Valve replacement dramatically improves survival at any symptomatic stage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','easy','flashcard','What valvular changes are seen in carcinoid heart disease and why are they right-sided?','["Carcinoid tumours (typically midgut/ileal) secrete serotonin, bradykinin, histamine, and other vasoactive substances. Right-sided cardiac valves are affected because: vasoactive substances from mesenteric carcinoids drain via the portal system → liver (which metabolises/inactivates serotonin by MAO) → hepatic veins → inferior vena cava → RIGHT heart before reaching the lungs (which metabolise remaining serotonin). Left heart is spared unless: (1) hepatic metastases allow direct systemic release; (2) pulmonary carcinoid; (3) right-to-left shunt. Pathological changes: fibrous plaques (serotonin-stimulated fibroblast proliferation) deposited on endocardial surface of tricuspid (TR) and pulmonic (PS) valves → leaflet thickening and retraction → tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonic stenosis."]','A','Right-sided = because lungs metabolise serotonin before it reaches the left heart. Serotonin causes fibrosis (via 5-HT2B receptors on fibroblasts). Same mechanism by which serotonin-releasing drugs (fenfluramine, methysergide) cause left-sided valve disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','medium','flashcard','What are the causes and mechanisms of aortic regurgitation?','["Primary valve disease: (1) Rheumatic fever (leaflet fibrosis/retraction — usually combined with AS); (2) Infective endocarditis (leaflet destruction); (3) Bicuspid aortic valve (congenital); (4) Calcific aortic degeneration (occasionally); (5) Rheumatoid arthritis (rarely). Aortic root disease: (1) Marfan syndrome (aortic root dilation → annular dilation); (2) Hypertension (aortic dilation); (3) Aortic dissection (Type A — disrupts aortic valve annulus); (4) Syphilitic aortitis (destroys annular support); (5) Ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis (aortitis). Mechanism in root disease: normal leaflets do not close because the annulus is too wide (''functional'' AR from root dilation without leaflet disease)."]','A','AR can arise from LEAFLET disease or AORTIC ROOT disease. Root causes (Marfan, dissection) affect normal leaflets by dilating the annulus. Mixed AR+AS = almost always rheumatic. Pure AR in young Marfan patient = think root dilation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','easy','flashcard','What are the three main cardiomyopathy patterns and their key features?','["1. Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM): Most common (90%); all four chambers dilated; poor systolic function (EF reduced); flabby, pale heart with mural thrombi; causes include genetic (cytoskeletal mutations), viral, alcohol, peripartum, idiopathic. 2. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM): Massive LVH (often asymmetric septal hypertrophy); hypercontracting but diastolic dysfunction; myocyte disarray; interstitial fibrosis; caused by sarcomere mutations (beta-MHC, MYBPC3); leading cause of SCD in young athletes. 3. Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM): Normal/near-normal sized ventricles; impaired diastolic filling from rigid walls; preserved systolic function; causes include amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, haemochromatosis, endomyocardial fibrosis."]','A','The 3 cardiomyopathies in one phrase: Dilated = big and weak. Hypertrophic = big and stiff (outflow obstruction in 1/3). Restrictive = normal size but stiff (can''t fill). All cause HF but by different mechanisms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','easy','flashcard','What is hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and what are the pathological findings?','["HCM is defined by massive myocardial hypertrophy in the absence of pressure or volume overload. Gross: Heavy heart (500-600g); predominantly asymmetric septal hypertrophy (septal-to-free-wall ratio >1.3:1); in some, the outflow tract is obstructed by systolic anterior motion (SAM) of the mitral valve → dynamic LVOTO in ~1/3. Microscopy: (1) Myocyte disarray (haphazard arrangement, branching) — PATHOGNOMONIC; (2) Interstitial fibrosis; (3) Thickened, dysplastic small intramural coronary arteries (medial hypertrophy). Genetics: autosomal dominant mutations in sarcomere genes: (1) Beta-MHC (MYH7, ~35%); (2) Myosin binding protein C (MYBPC3, ~35%); (3) Troponin T (TNNT2), troponin I, tropomyosin. All produce dysfunctional sarcomeres."]','A','Myocyte disarray = the histological smoking gun of HCM. The cardinal pathological triad: (1) asymmetric hypertrophy, (2) myocyte disarray, (3) interstitial fibrosis. These create the arrhythmogenic substrate for SCD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','flashcard','What genetic mutations cause dilated cardiomyopathy and how do cytoskeletal protein mutations impair function?','["DCM genetics (familial DCM = 20-35% of cases, AD inheritance): (1) Cytoskeletal proteins (most common): Dystrophin (links actin to extracellular matrix; X-linked; also causes muscular dystrophy); Desmin; Metavinculin; Sarcoglycan complex. Mechanism: cytoskeletal defects allow mechanical stress during contraction to tear the sarcolemma → Ca²+ influx → myocyte necrosis. (2) Sarcomere proteins (less common in DCM than HCM): actin, beta-MHC, troponin T. (3) Nuclear envelope: Lamin A/C (LMNA) mutations — DCM + conduction disease + muscular dystrophy (Emery-Dreifuss). (4) Mitochondrial mutations: impaired ATP production. (5) Other: titin (TTN) — most common genetic cause of DCM; desmoplakin."]','A','Titin (TTN) truncating mutations = most common identifiable genetic cause of DCM (~25% of familial DCM). Cytoskeletal mutations = mechanical coupling defect. Lamin A/C = think DCM + heart block + possible sudden death (high-risk — early ICD consideration).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','flashcard','What are the causes and pathological features of restrictive cardiomyopathy?','["Restrictive cardiomyopathy is caused by infiltration or fibrosis of the myocardium that increases myocardial stiffness, impairing diastolic filling. Causes: (1) Amyloidosis (most common in adults): AL amyloid (myeloma-associated) or ATTR (transthyretin, senile/familial). Congo red stain → apple-green birefringence under polarised light. Sparkling myocardium on echo. (2) Sarcoidosis: non-caseating granulomas → fibrosis, conduction disease. (3) Haemochromatosis: iron deposits in myocytes (Prussian blue stain). (4) Endomyocardial fibrosis (tropical, biventricular apex fibrosis + mural thrombus). (5) Loeffler endomyocarditis (hypereosinophilic syndrome — eosinophilic myocardial injury + mural thrombus). Haemodynamics: diastolic dysfunction with dip-and-plateau (''square root sign'') on pressure tracing; equalisation of diastolic pressures."]','A','RCM = stiff heart that cannot fill. Congo red = amyloid. Prussian blue = haemochromatosis iron. ''Sparkling'' echo appearance = amyloid deposits. ''Square root sign'' on haemodynamic tracing = hallmark of RCM (shared with constrictive pericarditis — hard to distinguish clinically).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','flashcard','What is arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC) and what are its key features?','["ARVC is characterised by progressive fibro-fatty replacement of right ventricular myocardium (± LV involvement). Genetics: autosomal dominant mutations in desmosomal proteins: desmoplakin (DSP), plakophilin-2 (PKP2) — most common, desmoglein-2, desmocollin-2, plakoglobin (causes Naxos disease). Pathogenesis: defective desmosomes → cardiomyocyte detachment during mechanical stress → cell death → replacement by fibro-fatty tissue. Pathology: right ventricular dilation and aneurysm, near-transmural fat and fibrosis in the RV free wall (''triangle of dysplasia'' — RV inflow, outflow, apex). ECG: epsilon wave (V1-V3), T-wave inversion in right precordial leads. Presentation: VT with LBBB morphology (arising from RV), SCD (exercise-triggered), RV failure."]','A','ARVC = right heart equivalent of DCM but caused by desmosome mutations. Desmosomes hold cardiomyocytes together — when defective, mechanical stress (exercise) pulls cells apart → death → fat and fibrosis. Key: LBBB-morphology VT from RV origin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','hard','flashcard','What are the main causes of myocarditis and what does the biopsy show?','["Viral (most common): Coxsackievirus B (most common in developed countries), adenovirus, CMV, HIV, parvovirus B19, HHV-6. Mechanism: direct viral cytotoxicity + immune-mediated (T-cell attack on antigen-mimicking cardiac proteins). Non-viral: (1) Bacterial: Corynebacterium diphtheriae (exotoxin inhibits protein synthesis in cardiomyocytes); (2) Protozoan: Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas disease) — amastigotes in acute phase, immune-mediated in chronic; (3) Parasitic: Trichinella spiralis (eosinophilic myocarditis). (4) Immune-mediated: Giant cell myocarditis (rare, fatal, idiopathic — giant cells + eosinophils + necrosis on biopsy); Hypersensitivity myocarditis (drug reaction — penicillins, sulfonamides; eosinophilic interstitial infiltrate). Dallas criteria: myocarditis on biopsy = inflammatory infiltrate adjacent to myocyte necrosis."]','A','Dallas criteria for biopsy diagnosis: lymphocytic infiltrate + adjacent myocyte necrosis/degeneration = active myocarditis. Giant cell myocarditis = most severe form, often requires heart transplantation or mechanical support.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','hard','flashcard','How does alcohol cause dilated cardiomyopathy?','["Alcoholic cardiomyopathy: Direct toxic effect of ethanol and its metabolite acetaldehyde on cardiomyocytes: (1) Mitochondrial dysfunction: ethanol/acetaldehyde impair oxidative phosphorylation → reduced ATP → contractile dysfunction; (2) Oxidative stress: increased ROS from acetaldehyde metabolism → lipid peroxidation and protein damage; (3) Calcium handling: impaired SERCA2a activity → diastolic dysfunction; (4) Thiamine (B1) deficiency in alcoholics → beriberi-associated high-output HF; (5) Direct myocyte apoptosis. Pathology: DCM pattern — all chambers dilated, poor systolic function. Recovery: partial-to-complete recovery of LV function possible with abstinence if disease is not too advanced (unlike ischaemic DCM where scar is irreversible)."]','A','Alcohol = direct cardiotoxin. Unique feature: alcoholic DCM is partially REVERSIBLE with abstinence (unlike ischaemic cardiomyopathy). This is why identifying and treating alcoholism is critical in these patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','easy','flashcard','What is the gross pathological appearance of the heart in dilated cardiomyopathy?','["In DCM the heart is enlarged and heavy (often >900g; normal ~300g). All four chambers are dilated (biventricular dilation), giving a globular appearance. The ventricular walls may appear thickened relative to body size but are actually thinned relative to the degree of dilation (eccentric hypertrophy — volume overload pattern). The myocardium appears pale and flabby. Mural thrombi are common, particularly in the LV apex and the atrial appendages (from stasis in dilated, poorly contractile chambers). On cut section, the walls are thin and the chambers are markedly dilated. Histology: non-specific findings — mild myocyte hypertrophy with increased nuclear size, interstitial and perivascular fibrosis, and occasional myocyte dropout."]','A','DCM heart = big, pale, and flabby — a ''bag of water''. Compare HCM: small chamber, thick wall, hypercontractile. The mural thrombi in DCM are a major source of systemic emboli (stroke, visceral ischaemia).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the major causes and types of pericarditis?','["Acute pericarditis types and causes: (1) Serous pericarditis: non-infectious inflammatory diseases — rheumatic fever, SLE, scleroderma, uraemia, tumour, drug reactions. (2) Fibrinous/serofibrinous pericarditis: most common type; causes include acute MI (1-3 days post-MI = epistenocardiac pericarditis), uraemia, viral infection, chest radiation, rheumatic fever, trauma. Gross: ''bread and butter'' appearance. (3) Purulent (suppurative) pericarditis: bacterial infection (S. aureus, Streptococcus, gram-negatives) reaching pericardium by direct spread, haematogenous seeding, or postoperative. (4) Haemorrhagic pericarditis: TB, malignancy, cardiac surgery, trauma. (5) Caseous pericarditis: TB (can lead to constrictive pericarditis). Primary pericarditis: almost always viral (Coxsackievirus B, echovirus) — most common cause overall."]','A','Type by gross appearance: serous (clear), fibrinous (''bread and butter''), purulent (pus), haemorrhagic (bloody). Key: viral pericarditis = most common primary cause; fibrinous = most common pattern seen in clinical practice (post-MI, uraemia, viral).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','easy','flashcard','What is cardiac tamponade and what determines haemodynamic compromise?','["Cardiac tamponade: compression of the heart by fluid in the pericardial sac, impeding diastolic filling of all chambers, reducing cardiac output. Key features: (1) Volume of fluid matters less than RATE of accumulation: 200-300 mL rapidly accumulated → tamponade; >1000 mL slowly accumulated → no tamponade (pericardium stretches). (2) The pericardium has limited compliance — once its compliance limit is reached, small additional volumes cause steep pressure rise. (3) All cardiac chambers are compressed equally → elevated and equalised diastolic filling pressures (RA = RV diastolic = PCWP = LA pressure). Clinical signs: Beck''s triad (hypotension, muffled heart sounds, raised JVP), pulsus paradoxus (>10 mmHg drop in systolic BP with inspiration). ECG: electrical alternans (QRS amplitude varies beat-to-beat from heart swinging in fluid)."]','A','Rate of accumulation > total volume for tamponade risk. 200 mL rapidly = tamponade; 1500 mL slowly = no tamponade (chronic effusion). Beck''s triad = Hypotension + Muffled sounds + JVP↑. Pulsus paradoxus = classic physiological sign.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is constrictive pericarditis and how does it differ clinically and haemodynamically from tamponade?','["Constrictive pericarditis: Dense fibrous or fibrocalcific pericardium rigidly encases the heart, limiting diastolic expansion of all chambers. Causes: TB (commonest worldwide), idiopathic, post-cardiac surgery, post-radiation (breast/lung/lymphoma), prior purulent/viral pericarditis. Haemodynamics: elevated and EQUALISED diastolic pressures in all chambers (like tamponade); diastolic dip-and-plateau (''square root sign'') on pressure tracings; enhanced respiratory variation in ventricular filling (exaggerated ventricular interdependence). Clinical features: elevated JVP with Kussmaul sign (JVP rises on inspiration — cannot transmit inspiratory pressure drop through rigid pericardium), peripheral oedema, ascites, hepatomegaly. Key distinction from tamponade: Kussmaul sign present in constriction; pulsus paradoxus less prominent; echo shows no large effusion but pericardial thickening/calcification; CT shows calcified pericardium."]','A','Tamponade vs Constriction: both equalise diastolic pressures. Tamponade → pulsus paradoxus. Constriction → Kussmaul sign. Both → ''diastolic equalisation''. ''P for Pulsus = Pressure (tamPonade); K for Kussmaul = Konstriction.''','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the outcomes of pericarditis — how can it heal and what are the chronic sequelae?','["Resolution: Most acute pericarditis (especially viral) resolves completely with minimal or no residual scarring. Organisation: Fibrinous exudate undergoes organisation → fibrous adhesions. Focal: Plaque-like fibrous thickenings (''soldier''s plaques'') — incidental, no haemodynamic consequence. More extensive: Mesh-like adhesions obliterating the pericardial sac (adhesive pericarditis) — usually no functional impairment if the pericardium remains pliable. Adhesive mediastinopericarditis: Pericardial sac obliterated AND adherent to surrounding mediastinal structures; each systolic contraction pulls against adherent structures (Broadbent sign — systolic retraction of rib cage and diaphragm); increases cardiac workload → hypertrophy and dilation. Constrictive pericarditis: Dense, fibrous or calcified pericardium rigidly restricts cardiac filling; TB and radiation are classic causes."]','A','Pericarditis outcomes: resolve → no scar; mild fibrous adhesions → soldier''s plaques (incidental); constrictive pericarditis → serious haemodynamic compromise. TB → most likely to cause constrictive pericarditis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is haemopericardium and what are the common causes?','["Haemopericardium (blood in the pericardial sac) causes: (1) Cardiac rupture from MI: most common cause; free wall rupture 3-7 days post-MI → haemopericardium → acute tamponade → rapid death. (2) Aortic dissection Type A: dissection extending retrograde to the pericardium → haemopericardium. (3) Trauma: penetrating cardiac injury (stab wound, gunshot); blunt thoracic trauma. (4) Iatrogenic: pacemaker lead perforation, central line placement, cardiac catheterisation, cardiac surgery. (5) Aortic aneurysm rupture into pericardium. (6) Malignancy: tumour eroding cardiac muscle or pericardium. Rapidly accumulating haemopericardium (200-300 mL) → acute tamponade; slowly accumulating → larger volumes tolerated before haemodynamic compromise."]','A','Key haemopericardium scenarios: Post-MI day 3-7 + acute hypotension = free wall rupture → haemopericardium. Marfan + Type A dissection = haemopericardium. Both require emergency pericardiocentesis and surgery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','hard','flashcard','What are the pericardial manifestations of systemic diseases?','["SLE: Pericarditis is the most common cardiac manifestation of SLE (~25%); usually fibrinous or serous; may be associated with Libman-Sacks endocarditis (simultaneous valvular disease). Uraemia: Fibrinous or haemorrhagic pericarditis from uraemic toxins (urate crystal deposition on pericardium); dialysis-related pericarditis (from dialysis fluid). Rheumatoid arthritis: Fibrinous pericarditis; less common than in SLE. Cancer: Malignant pericardial effusion (haemorrhagic exudate); lung > breast > melanoma. Tuberculosis: Fibrinous → organising → constrictive; caseous necrosis may be present. Hypothyroidism: Pericardial effusion from accumulation of protein-rich fluid (mucin) — ''myxoedema heart''; heart appears enlarged on CXR but function is usually preserved. Post-cardiac injury syndrome (Dressler syndrome): autoimmune fibrinous pericarditis 2-10 weeks after MI or cardiac surgery."]','A','Systemic disease → pericardium: SLE = most common (fibrinous); Uraemia = haemorrhagic; TB = constrictive (long-term); Hypothyroidism = large serous effusion (mucin-rich); Malignancy = haemorrhagic exudate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','easy','flashcard','What is Dressler syndrome and how is it distinguished from epistenocardiac pericarditis?','["Epistenocardiac pericarditis (early post-MI pericarditis): Occurs 1-3 DAYS post-MI. Mechanism: fibrinous inflammation of the epicardium directly overlying the infarct zone from necrotic tissue contact. Localised to the area over the infarct. Resolves within days. Does not recur. Dressler syndrome (late post-MI pericarditis): Occurs 2-10 WEEKS post-MI (or post-cardiac surgery = post-pericardiotomy syndrome). Mechanism: autoimmune — antibodies develop against cardiac antigens released during necrosis (myosin, cardiac troponin). Features: fever, pleuritic chest pain, pericardial and pleural effusion, elevated ESR. Responds to NSAIDs and colchicine. May recur."]','A','Timing distinguishes them: Epistenocardiac = DAYS 1-3 (local, direct); Dressler = WEEKS 2-10 (autoimmune, systemic). Dressler can also follow cardiac surgery (post-pericardiotomy syndrome). Both cause friction rub and ST changes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','hard','flashcard','How does pericardial constraint affect ventricular interdependence and what is the clinical significance?','["Ventricular interdependence: The two ventricles share the interventricular septum and are enclosed within the pericardial sac (fixed total pericardial volume). In normal breathing: inspiration reduces intrathoracic pressure → increased venous return to RV → RV expands → septum shifts slightly toward LV → mild reduction in LV filling (normal pulsus paradoxus <10 mmHg). In cardiac tamponade: Pericardial fluid compresses all chambers; inspiration increases RV filling (venous return) but total pericardial volume cannot increase → RV expansion pushes septum toward LV (exaggerated leftward septal shift) → LV filling falls → systolic BP drops >10 mmHg = pulsus paradoxus. In constrictive pericarditis: Rigid pericardium prevents intrathoracic pressure transmission to cardiac chambers → loss of normal inspiratory increase in right heart filling → JVP fails to fall and paradoxically rises (Kussmaul sign)."]','A','Pericardial constraint = the rigid ''room'' the heart lives in. In tamponade, that room is too full → ventricular competition (interdependence) is exaggerated → pulsus paradoxus. In constriction, the walls are rigid → inspiratory pressure changes cannot enter → Kussmaul.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the ECG changes in acute pericarditis and how are they distinguished from MI?','["Acute pericarditis ECG: (1) Diffuse saddle-shaped (concave upward) ST elevation in MULTIPLE leads (not restricted to a coronary territory); (2) PR depression (very specific for pericarditis); (3) No reciprocal ST depression (unlike MI which shows ST elevation in one territory + reciprocal changes in opposite leads); (4) T-wave inversion develops AFTER ST has returned to baseline (unlike MI where T-wave inversion accompanies or precedes ST normalisation). ECG evolves through 4 stages over days-weeks. Pericarditis vs STEMI key distinctions: STEMI = convex (tombstone) ST elevation, reciprocal changes, specific territory, Q waves develop; Pericarditis = concave ST elevation, no reciprocal changes, diffuse leads, PR depression, no Q waves."]','A','PR depression = most specific ECG finding for pericarditis. Saddle-shaped (concave) ST elevation in multiple leads, no Q waves, no reciprocal changes = pericarditis. Tombstone (convex) ST elevation + Q waves + reciprocal changes = STEMI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the significance of pericardial effusion size and how is it classified?','["Pericardial effusion classification by volume: Small: <100 mL (echo: posterior strip only). Moderate: 100-500 mL (echo: circumferential). Large: >500 mL (echo: swinging heart; CXR: flask-shaped cardiac shadow >250 mL). Important principle: Rate of accumulation > total volume for haemodynamic consequences. Fluid types by appearance: Transudate (clear/straw): HF, hypoalbuminaemia, hypothyroidism. Exudate (cloudy, high protein): infection, malignancy, TB, inflammatory. Haemorrhagic: trauma, MI rupture, dissection, TB, malignancy. Chylous (milky): lymphatic obstruction. Slowly accumulating: pericardium dilates, tolerating large volumes (up to 1-2 L) without tamponade. Rapidly accumulating: Even 200-300 mL can cause tamponade (pericardium has no time to stretch)."]','A','Flask-shaped heart on CXR = think large pericardial effusion (or massive cardiomegaly). The pericardium can stretch slowly to accommodate large volumes — the danger is RATE of accumulation, not total volume.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','easy','flashcard','How are congenital heart defects classified as cyanotic vs acyanotic?','["Acyanotic (left-to-right shunts initially — no cyanosis unless Eisenmenger occurs): (1) VSD (most common CHD, 25-30%); (2) ASD (5-10%); (3) PDA; (4) Atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD); (5) Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return. Cyanotic: Right-to-left shunts (deoxygenated blood enters systemic circulation — early/congenital cyanosis): (1) Tetralogy of Fallot (most common cyanotic CHD in children beyond infancy); (2) Transposition of great arteries (most common cyanotic CHD presenting at birth); (3) Tricuspid atresia; (4) Total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR); (5) Truncus arteriosus; (6) Hypoplastic left heart syndrome. Obstructive: Pulmonary stenosis, aortic stenosis, coarctation of the aorta (no shunt, no cyanosis, but outflow obstruction)."]','A','Acyanotic = pink = L→R shunt. Cyanotic = blue = R→L shunt. Most common CHD overall = VSD. Most common CYANOTIC in newborn = TGA. Most common cyanotic BEYOND infancy = Tetralogy of Fallot. High-yield list for exams.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','easy','flashcard','What are the key features of atrial septal defect (ASD)?','["ASD types by location: (1) Secundum ASD (70%): in the region of the fossa ovalis; most common; often isolated; associated with maternal alcohol use. (2) Primum ASD (15%): adjacent to AV valves; associated with Down syndrome and AV septal defect. (3) Sinus venosus ASD (10%): near SVC entry; associated with partial anomalous pulmonary venous return. Haemodynamics: L→R shunt → right heart volume overload → RV dilation and tricuspid dilation → right axis deviation + RBBB on ECG. Auscultation: systolic ejection murmur (increased flow across pulmonary valve) + fixed split S2 (ASD prevents normal respiratory variation in pulmonary venous return to LA). Complications: Eisenmenger syndrome (long-term), paradoxical embolism (with R→L after Eisenmenger or with Valsalva), atrial arrhythmias. Patent foramen ovale (PFO) is separate: probe-patent in 25% of adults; not a true ASD but can allow paradoxical embolism."]','A','Fixed split S2 = pathognomonic of ASD. ''Fixed'' = S2 split does NOT change with respiration (normal S2 split widens with inspiration, narrows with expiration). ASD prevents this respiratory variation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','flashcard','What are the four components of Tetralogy of Fallot and how do they arise embryologically?','["Four components: (1) Pulmonary stenosis (RVOTO — subvalvular/infundibular > valvular); (2) Ventricular septal defect (large, perimembranous); (3) Overriding aorta (aorta positioned directly over the VSD, receiving blood from both ventricles); (4) Right ventricular hypertrophy (compensatory, from RV pressure overload fighting stenotic pulmonary valve). Embryological basis: Anterior deviation of the infundibular (conal) septum during cardiac development → septum shifts anteriorly → narrows RV outflow tract (causing pulmonary stenosis) + creates VSD (fails to align with muscular septum) + aorta overrides VSD. All four features arise from this SINGLE embryological defect. Correction: complete surgical repair at 6-12 months (VSD patch + right ventricular outflow tract augmentation)."]','A','All 4 features = 1 embryological defect (anterior deviation of infundibular septum). The RVH is SECONDARY (consequence of pulmonary stenosis) not a primary defect. PROS: only 4 components, single embryological cause — very logical.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','flashcard','What is Eisenmenger syndrome and how does it develop?','["Eisenmenger syndrome: Reversal of a previously left-to-right shunt to right-to-left due to development of severe pulmonary hypertension from pulmonary vascular disease. Pathogenesis: Large L→R shunt (VSD, ASD, PDA) → chronic pulmonary volume/pressure overload → reactive changes in pulmonary vessels: (1) Medial hypertrophy of pulmonary arteries; (2) Intimal fibrosis and proliferation; (3) Plexiform lesions (in severe long-standing cases) — dilated angiomatoid vessels arising from pulmonary arterioles. Eventually pulmonary vascular resistance exceeds systemic → shunt REVERSES to R→L → cyanosis develops. Clinical features: Cyanosis, polycythaemia (from chronic hypoxaemia → EPO → increased RBCs), digital clubbing, paradoxical embolism, haemoptysis. Contraindications: Eisenmenger syndrome contraindicates closure of the shunt (would cause acute severe RV failure)."]','A','Eisenmenger = the point of no return for uncorrected L→R shunts. Once pulmonary vascular disease is established (Eisenmenger), shunt closure is CONTRAINDICATED. Prevention = early surgical repair before pulmonary hypertension develops (usually before age 2).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','flashcard','What is patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) and what maintains ductal patency vs closure?','["The ductus arteriosus connects the main pulmonary artery to the descending aorta (distal to the left subclavian) — in foetal life it carries ~90% of RV output directly to the aorta (bypassing the fluid-filled, high-resistance lungs). Normal closure: Initiated by: (1) Rising pO2 at birth → direct ductal smooth muscle constriction; (2) Falling PGE2 (from falling placental production and increased pulmonary metabolism of PGE2). Anatomical closure: Within 1-3 months (fibrosis → ligamentum arteriosum). PDA in premature infants: High circulating PGE2 + relative hypoxaemia → failure of ductal closure. Treatment: indomethacin or ibuprofen (COX inhibitor → ↓PGE2 → ductal closure) or surgical ligation. PDA maintains pulmonary artery-aorta connection, creating a L→R shunt (aortic pressure > PA) → continuous ''machinery'' murmur. Prostaglandin E1 infusion maintains ductal patency in ductal-dependent lesions (e.g., pulmonary atresia, severe coarctation)."]','A','PDA closure = high O2 + low PGE2. PDA maintenance = PGE1 infusion. PDA treatment in premature = indomethacin (COX inhibitor). The ''machinery'' murmur is continuous (systole AND diastole) because aortic pressure always exceeds PA pressure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','hard','flashcard','What are the genetics of congenital heart disease and which syndromes are associated with specific defects?','["Key genetic associations: Trisomy 21 (Down): AVSD/AVCD (40%), VSD, ASD — most common CHD. Trisomy 18 (Edwards): VSD, polyvalvular disease, PDA. Trisomy 13 (Patau): VSD, ASD, PDA. Turner syndrome (45,X): Coarctation of aorta (most common CHD), bicuspid aortic valve, AS. Marfan syndrome (FBN1): Aortic root dilation, AR, mitral valve prolapse. Williams syndrome (chromosome 7q11 deletion): Supravalvular aortic stenosis (most specific CHD). Noonan syndrome (RAS-MAPK mutations): Pulmonary stenosis, HCM. DiGeorge syndrome (22q11 deletion — velocardiofacial syndrome): Conotruncal defects (Tetralogy of Fallot, truncus arteriosus, interrupted aortic arch) + immunodeficiency + hypoparathyroidism. Transcription factors: NKX2.5 → ASD + AV block; TBX5 → Holt-Oram (ASD/VSD + limb defects); GATA4 → various septal defects."]','A','The ''CATCH 22'' of DiGeorge syndrome: Cardiac defects, Abnormal facies, T-cell immunodeficiency, Cleft palate, Hypocalcaemia (hypoparathyroidism) — caused by 22q11 deletion. Most common cause of conotruncal defects.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','hard','flashcard','What is the embryological basis of transposition of the great arteries (TGA) and why is it incompatible with life without mixing?','["TGA arises from failure of the spiral rotation of the aortopulmonary septum during cardiac development (weeks 5-8). Normally the septum spirals to connect the LV (systemic) to the aorta and the RV (pulmonary) to the PA. In TGA, the septum develops without spiralling → aorta arises from the RV (carries deoxygenated blood to body) and PA arises from LV (carries oxygenated blood back to lungs). Two circuits in PARALLEL (not in series): systemic deoxygenated blood circulates body → RV → aorta → body (never oxygenated). Pulmonary oxygenated blood circulates lungs → LV → PA → lungs (never reaches body). Incompatible with life without mixing: requires ASD, VSD, or PDA to allow oxygenated blood into systemic circulation. Emergency: prostaglandin E1 (keep PDA open) + balloon atrial septostomy (Rashkind). Definitive: arterial switch operation (Jatene) in first week."]','A','TGA = the two circuits are independent parallel loops. Survival requires mixing. Fix: arterial switch operation (physically swapping aorta and PA to their correct ventricles) must be done within 2 weeks while LV is still able to support systemic pressures.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','easy','flashcard','What is coarctation of the aorta and what are its associations?','["Coarctation = localised narrowing of the aorta, most commonly at the aortic isthmus (junction of aortic arch and descending aorta, at the insertion of the ligamentum arteriosum). Two classical types (historical): (1) Pre-ductal (infantile): proximal to ligamentum arteriosum; associated with other severe CHD; often ductal-dependent; presents with cyanosis (differential cyanosis — blue lower body, pink upper body if ductus right-to-left); (2) Post-ductal (adult): distal to ligamentum arteriosum; collateral circulation (intercostal arteries) develops; presents in older children/adults. Clinical features: Upper limb hypertension + weak/delayed femoral pulses, pressure gradient between arms and legs, rib notching on CXR (3rd-9th ribs, inferior surface), hypertension above coarctation. Associations: bicuspid aortic valve (50%), Turner syndrome (25-30% have coarctation), Berry aneurysms of the circle of Willis."]','A','Coarctation signs: ''Radio-femoral delay''; rib notching on CXR; upper limb hypertension. Strong association with bicuspid aortic valve and Turner syndrome. Rib notching develops ONLY after years of collateral flow (3rd-9th ribs).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','easy','flashcard','What are the most common primary cardiac tumours in adults and children?','["Primary cardiac tumours are RARE (prevalence ~0.002%). Benign tumours account for 80-90%: Adults (in order of frequency): (1) Myxoma (most common; 50% of benign tumours); (2) Lipoma; (3) Papillary fibroelastoma; (4) Rhabdomyoma (2nd most common in adults overall but #1 in children); (5) Fibroma. Malignant primary tumours: (1) Angiosarcoma (most common primary malignant cardiac tumour; right atrium); (2) Rhabdomyosarcoma; (3) Mesothelioma (pericardium). Children: Rhabdomyoma (most common, strongly associated with tuberous sclerosis); Fibroma (2nd). Note: Metastatic tumours are FAR more common than primary cardiac tumours (metastases in ~5% of cancer patients vs primary cardiac tumours in 0.002% of population)."]','A','Memory: ''Myxoma is most common in ADULTS. Rhabdomyoma in CHILDREN (+ tuberous sclerosis). Angiosarcoma = most common malignant. Metastatic > Primary overall.'' The ratio metastases:primary tumours is approximately 20:1.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','easy','flashcard','What are the clinical features and pathological characteristics of cardiac myxoma?','["Myxoma: Most common primary cardiac tumour in adults. Location: 90% in atria; L:R ratio 4:1; attached to fossa ovalis region of interatrial septum by a stalk. Gross: Gelatinous, polypoid or papillary, glistening, fragile mass (1-10 cm). Microscopy: Primitive mesenchymal cells (''lepidic'' or ''stellate'' myxoma cells with round nuclei) embedded in abundant myxoid stroma rich in acid mucopolysaccharides; surface covered by endothelium; blood vessels and haemorrhage present. Clinical presentation — three syndromes: (1) Obstruction: simulates mitral stenosis (tumour prolapses through MV in diastole) → dyspnoea, ''tumour plop'' (low-pitched diastolic sound), positional symptoms (changing body position changes the symptoms). (2) Embolism: tumour fragments or thrombus from surface embolise → stroke, peripheral emboli. (3) Constitutional: fever, weight loss, elevated ESR/CRP (tumour secretes IL-6) — simulates endocarditis/vasculitis."]','A','Myxoma triad: Obstruction + Emboli + Constitutional symptoms. The ''tumour plop'' is pathognomonic. Myxoma = the great imitator of mitral stenosis but with emboli and systemic symptoms. Surgical excision is curative; recurrence rate ~3% sporadic, higher in Carney complex.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','flashcard','What is non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis (NBTE) and what conditions predispose to it?','["NBTE (marantic endocarditis): Sterile, bland, non-destructive vegetations on any cardiac valve, deposited along the line of valve closure. Composition: fibrin and platelets only — no organisms, no significant inflammatory cells. Size: small (1-5 mm); flat or slightly raised. Unlike infective endocarditis: no valve destruction; no microorganisms; no inflammatory infiltrate. Predisposing conditions: (1) Prolonged debilitating illnesses — cancer (especially mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas: pancreas, lung, colon — hypercoagulability + thrombocytosis); (2) Sepsis; (3) Burns; (4) Malnutrition; (5) Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome; (6) Hypercoagulable states. Clinical significance: Despite being sterile and small, these vegetations embolise and cause strokes and systemic ischaemia. Term ''marantic'' (from Greek: marasmus = wasting) reflects the debilitated state of affected patients."]','A','NBTE = clean, sterile, small vegetations that still cause strokes. Think of mucin-producing adenocarcinomas (pancreas, lung, stomach) = classic association. The mucin activates clotting (Trousseau syndrome = migratory thrombophlebitis from same mechanism).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','flashcard','What is Carney complex and how does it relate to cardiac myxoma?','["Carney complex is a rare autosomal dominant syndrome caused by loss-of-function mutations in PRKAR1A (encoding a regulatory subunit of cAMP-dependent protein kinase). It is characterised by: (1) Cardiac myxomas (may be multiple, bilateral, recur after surgery); (2) Non-cardiac myxomas (skin, breast); (3) Skin pigmentation abnormalities (spotty, lentigines, blue naevi, cafe-au-lait spots); (4) Endocrine tumours (primary pigmented nodular adrenocortical disease → Cushing syndrome; GH-secreting pituitary adenomas; thyroid tumours); (5) Testicular tumours (large cell calcifying Sertoli cell tumours). Cardiac myxoma in Carney complex: younger age, multiple, bilateral (can be in any chamber), high recurrence rate. Also related: McCune-Albright syndrome (activating GNAS1 mutation → Gsα) can also cause myxomas."]','A','Carney complex = PRKAR1A mutation → myxomas (multiple, recurrent) + pigmented skin lesions + endocrine tumours. Any young patient with recurrent or bilateral myxomas should be evaluated for Carney complex and genetic testing offered.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','flashcard','How do intracardiac thrombi form and what are the consequences by location?','["Intracardiac thrombi form by Virchow''s triad: (1) Stasis; (2) Endothelial/endocardial injury; (3) Hypercoagulability. By location: Left atrial appendage (LAA): Atrial fibrillation → loss of atrial contraction → stasis in LAA (trabeculated, narrow-necked) → thrombus → systemic emboli (stroke in 90% of AF-related cardioembolic events). Left ventricular apex: Post-MI (large anterior/apical) → akinetic/dyskinetic myocardium + endocardial injury + post-MI hypercoagulability → mural thrombus → systemic emboli (stroke, limb ischaemia). Right atrial/ventricular: Deep vein thrombus may embolise to right heart → pulmonary emboli; or in setting of R→L shunt → paradoxical embolism → stroke. Ventricular aneurysm: Chronic stasis in aneurysm → organised laminated thrombus → chronic embolic risk."]','A','LAA thrombus = AF → stroke. LV apex thrombus = anterior MI → stroke/embolism. Both require anticoagulation. TEE (transoesophageal echo) is best for LAA thrombus detection (TTE has poor sensitivity). LV thrombus seen on TTE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','hard','flashcard','What are the features of cardiac angiosarcoma and what distinguishes it from benign vascular tumours?','["Cardiac angiosarcoma is the most common primary MALIGNANT cardiac tumour. Key features: Location: Right atrium (most common); may involve pericardium. Gross: Poorly circumscribed, haemorrhagic, necrotic mass with irregular infiltrative borders. Microscopy: Atypical endothelial cells forming irregular, anastomosing vascular channels; high mitotic rate; cytological atypia; areas of solid growth (poorly differentiated areas have no recognisable vessel formation); tumour cells positive for CD31, CD34, ERG (endothelial markers). Distinguishing from benign haemangioma: Haemangioma = well-circumscribed, normal-appearing endothelium, no atypia, no mitoses, organised vascular channels. Angiosarcoma = poorly circumscribed, cytological atypia, mitoses, necrosis, irregular/anastomosing channels, locally invasive, early metastases (lung, liver, brain). Prognosis: Very poor; median survival <1 year even with surgery + chemotherapy."]','A','Angiosarcoma vs haemangioma: the general rule for vascular tumours — well-circumscribed + normal endothelium + no atypia = benign. Irregular + atypia + mitoses + necrosis + invasive = malignant. Right atrium location is a red flag for malignant primary cardiac tumour.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','hard','flashcard','What cardiac effects do non-cardiac neoplasms have?','["Non-cardiac tumours affect the heart by: (1) Pericardial metastases (most common): haemorrhagic pericardial effusion/tamponade. Primary sites: lung, breast, melanoma, lymphoma, renal cell carcinoma. (2) Myocardial metastases: from leukaemia, lymphoma, melanoma, sarcomas. (3) Intracavitary extension (''tumour thrombus''): renal cell carcinoma extends via IVC → right atrium (classic). Hepatocellular carcinoma also extends via hepatic veins → IVC → RA. (4) Paraneoplastic effects: NBTE (marantic endocarditis) from mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas; carcinoid heart disease (serotonin from ileal carcinoids → right-sided valve fibrosis); (5) Cardiac effects of cancer treatment: anthracycline cardiotoxicity (DCM from free radical damage + topoisomerase II inhibition in cardiomyocytes); radiation-induced pericarditis, valve disease, coronary artery disease, conduction abnormalities."]','A','Cardiac effects of cancer: DIRECT (metastases, direct extension), INDIRECT (paraneoplastic: NBTE, carcinoid), TREATMENT-RELATED (anthracycline DCM, radiation). Renal cell carcinoma = classic ''tumour thrombus'' in IVC/RA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','easy','flashcard','What is rhabdomyoma of the heart and why is it strongly associated with tuberous sclerosis?','["Cardiac rhabdomyoma is the most common cardiac tumour in INFANTS AND CHILDREN. It is a hamartoma (not a true neoplasm) of cardiac muscle cells. Association with tuberous sclerosis: ~50% of patients with tuberous sclerosis have cardiac rhabdomyomas; conversely, most rhabdomyomas are associated with tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2 mutations → mTOR pathway overactivation → hamartoma growth). Gross: Multiple (50% are multiple), grey-white, firm nodules in the ventricular myocardium. Microscopy: Large, glycogen-laden cells (vacuolated) with cytoplasmic processes radiating from a central nucleus — ''spider cells'' (pathognomonic). Clinical: Can obstruct outflow tracts, cause arrhythmias, or simulate a valvular mass. Natural history: Most REGRESS SPONTANEOUSLY in childhood — observation is appropriate in most cases (unlike fibromas which do not regress)."]','A','Rhabdomyoma = tuberous sclerosis + spider cells + spontaneous regression. Fibroma = not associated with TS, does NOT regress, may need surgery. Both are benign ventricular tumours in children, but management differs.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','easy','flashcard','What is the definition of an aortic aneurysm and how are they classified?','["Aortic aneurysm: Localised abnormal dilation of the aorta >150% of the expected normal diameter (generally >3.0 cm for abdominal aorta; >5 cm = clinically significant AAA). Classification by morphology: (1) Fusiform: symmetrical dilation of the entire circumference (most common in atherosclerotic AAA). (2) Saccular: spherical outpouching of one wall (classic in syphilitic aortitis and mycotic aneurysms). Classification by location: (1) Abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA): usually infrarenal (between renal arteries and bifurcation); most common; caused by atherosclerosis. (2) Thoracic aortic aneurysm: ascending (syphilis, Marfan, hypertension, bicuspid AV); arch (atherosclerosis); descending (atherosclerosis + hypertension). Classification by wall composition: True aneurysm (all three wall layers intact); Pseudoaneurysm (false aneurysm — wall breach, contained by adventitia and/or periadventitial tissue only)."]','A','Aneurysm morphology: Fusiform = atherosclerotic. Saccular = syphilitic/mycotic. Location: AAA = infrarenal (most common). Thoracic ascending = Marfan/syphilis/HTN. True vs false: both contained but by different layers.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','easy','flashcard','What are the risk factors, pathogenesis, and complications of abdominal aortic aneurysm?','["AAA risk factors: Male > female (5:1), age >60, smoking (major modifiable risk), hypertension, family history, atherosclerosis. Rare before age 50. Pathogenesis: Severe atherosclerosis → ischaemia of aortic media (via obstruction of vasa vasorum) + inflammatory cell recruitment (macrophages, neutrophils) → MMP-9, MMP-12 secretion → elastin and collagen degradation → medial thinning and weakness → dilation. Also: SMC loss (apoptosis), reduced elastin synthesis. Morphology: Usually infrarenal; 15 cm long, up to 15 cm diameter; contains laminated mural thrombus; severe atherosclerosis of the wall. Complications: (1) Rupture (most dangerous, retroperitoneal > free peritoneal haemorrhage); (2) Thromboembolism from mural thrombus (blue toe syndrome); (3) Impingement on adjacent structures (ureter, duodenum, IVC); (4) Dissection (rare in AAA, more common in thoracic). Risk of rupture: >5 cm = significant; >5.5 cm = high risk → surgical repair (open vs EVAR)."]','A','AAA rule of 5s: >5 cm = consider repair; >5.5 cm = repair recommended; expansion rate >1 cm/year = repair regardless of size. Smoking cessation + blood pressure control slow expansion. EVAR (endovascular aortic repair) = less invasive alternative to open surgery in appropriate anatomy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','flashcard','What is aortic dissection and how is it classified by the Stanford system?','["Aortic dissection: Blood from the aortic lumen enters and separates the media through an intimal tear, creating a false lumen within the aortic wall. If the false lumen ruptures through the adventitia → death. The intimal tear is usually transverse. Stanford classification: Type A: Involves the ASCENDING aorta (regardless of where the tear originates or whether descending is also involved). Surgical emergency — rupture into pericardium (tamponade), coronary ostia (MI), aortic valve (AR). Type B: Involves ONLY the descending aorta (distal to left subclavian). Usually managed medically (blood pressure control) unless complications (malperfusion, expansion, rupture). DeBakey classification: I = ascending + descending; II = ascending only; III = descending only. Incidence: Peak in 60s-70s; males > females."]','A','Stanford A = Ascending → needs surgery. Stanford B = Beyond left subclavian → manage medically (antihypertensives + beta-blockers to reduce shear stress). Mnemonic: Type A = ''All the way'' or ''Ascending'' = surgery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','flashcard','What are the predisposing conditions for aortic dissection and what does the aortic wall show histologically?','["Most important predisposing conditions: (1) Hypertension (most common — accounts for 70% of dissections; chronic haemodynamic stress accelerates medial degeneration); (2) Cystic medial necrosis: Marfan syndrome (FBN1 mutation → defective fibrillin-1 → elastin assembly defect), Ehlers-Danlos (collagen mutations), bicuspid aortic valve (aortic root dilation + medial degeneration), Loeys-Dietz syndrome (TGFBR mutation). (3) Pregnancy (3rd trimester — cardiovascular changes + possible hormonal effects on aortic wall). (4) Cocaine use (acute severe hypertension). Histology: Cystic medial necrosis = pools of mucoid (myxoid) matrix within the media, fragmentation and loss of elastic fibres, SMC drop-out. These create planes of weakness within the media through which dissection propagates. The intimal tear occurs where haemodynamic stress concentrates: ascending aorta (within 10 cm of aortic valve) in Type A; aortic isthmus in Type B."]','A','Medial degeneration = prerequisite for dissection. Hypertension = most common cause. Marfan = prototypic genetic cause. Without medial disease, the aorta is too strong to dissect. This is why connective tissue disorders and hypertension dramatically increase dissection risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','flashcard','What are the clinical presentation and immediate complications of aortic dissection?','["Presentation: Sudden onset tearing/ripping chest pain, maximum at onset (classically described as the worst pain of their life), radiating to the back or between the shoulder blades. Associated features: hypertension (in Type B) or hypotension (in Type A with tamponade/AR). Blood pressure discrepancy between arms (>20 mmHg). New aortic regurgitation murmur. Neurological symptoms if carotid arteries involved. Complications of Type A: (1) Haemopericardium → cardiac tamponade (rapid death — most common cause of death in Type A); (2) Coronary ostia shearing → MI (usually RCA → inferior MI); (3) Aortic valve disruption → acute severe AR → LV failure; (4) Carotid artery involvement → stroke; (5) Subclavian artery involvement → arm ischaemia/pulse deficit. Complications of Type B: (1) Renal artery → acute renal failure; (2) Mesenteric artery → bowel ischaemia; (3) Iliac/femoral → leg ischaemia; (4) Rupture → haemorrhage."]','A','Type A dissection = aortic emergency — rush to OR. The triad of death in Type A: tamponade + acute MI + acute AR. Pain that is maximum at ONSET (unlike MI where pain builds to maximum) is the classic dissection symptom.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','hard','flashcard','What is a mycotic aneurysm and how does it form?','["Mycotic aneurysm: An aneurysm caused by infectious (microbial) destruction of the arterial wall. ''Mycotic'' is a misnomer — coined historically (most are bacterial, not fungal). Pathogenesis — three mechanisms: (1) Septic emboli: organisms (usually from infective endocarditis) lodge in the vasa vasorum or the vessel wall → suppurative inflammation → medial destruction → aneurysm. (2) Extension from adjacent infection: e.g., vertebral osteomyelitis extending to adjacent aorta; periaortic abscess. (3) Direct bacteraemia: circulating bacteria (e.g., Salmonella species) seed the aortic wall, especially at sites of pre-existing atherosclerosis. Most common organisms: Staphylococcus aureus (most common), Salmonella (classic for aortic), Streptococcus, gram-negative rods. Location: Any artery, but cerebral arteries, aorta, and iliofemoral arteries most common. Risk: Rapid expansion, rupture, and sepsis; requires antibiotics and often surgical/endovascular repair."]','A','Mycotic aneurysm = infected aneurysm (bacterial usually). Classic boards question: Salmonella aortitis → mycotic abdominal aortic aneurysm. IE → septic emboli → cerebral mycotic aneurysms (presents with headache or intracerebral haemorrhage).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','hard','flashcard','How does syphilitic aortitis cause aortic disease and what are its characteristic pathological findings?','["Tertiary syphilis (occurring 10-30 years after primary infection with Treponema pallidum): Treponema invades the aortic adventitia → obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum (inflammatory destruction of the nutrient vessels supplying the aortic media) → ischaemia of the aortic media → loss of elastic fibres and smooth muscle cells → medial scarring and weakening → progressive dilation. Pathological features: (1) Gross: ''Tree bark'' or wrinkled appearance of the aortic intima from scarring and contraction of underlying media; saccular aneurysm (usually of ascending thoracic aorta and arch); aortic annular dilation → aortic regurgitation. (2) Micro: Obliterative endarteritis with lymphoplasmacytic perivascular infiltrate of vasa vasorum; intimal fibrous thickening; medial loss of elastic tissue. (3) Complications: Aortic regurgitation (causing cor bovinum — ''ox heart'' from LV hypertrophy); coronary ostial stenosis → angina/MI; aneurysm rupture."]','A','Syphilitic aortitis hallmarks: ''tree bark'' aortic intima + saccular THORACIC (ascending) aneurysm + aortic regurgitation + coronary ostial stenosis. Distinguished from atherosclerotic AAA (infrarenal, fusiform) by location and morphology.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','flashcard','What are the different types of thoracic aortic aneurysm and their underlying causes?','["Ascending aortic aneurysm: Most common cause = cystic medial necrosis (Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, bicuspid AV); also hypertension (accelerated medial degeneration), syphilis (saccular, tree bark intima), and aortitis (giant cell arteritis, Takayasu). Aortic root aneurysm (annuloaortic ectasia): Classic in Marfan syndrome; the entire aortic root dilates giving a ''tulip'' or ''pear-shaped'' aortic root; causes aortic regurgitation from annular dilation; risk of dissection. Arch aneurysm: Usually atherosclerotic; may cause tracheal/oesophageal compression (dysphagia, dyspnoea, hoarseness from recurrent laryngeal nerve compression). Descending thoracic aneurysm: Predominantly atherosclerosis + hypertension; fusiform. All thoracic aneurysms are at higher risk of dissection compared to AAA. Management: elective repair when >5.5 cm (or >5.0 cm in Marfan)."]','A','Thoracic ascending aneurysm = think Marfan or syphilis. Aortic root dilation + AR + tall young patient = Marfan (fibrillin-1 mutation). In Marfan: beta-blockers and losartan slow aortic root expansion; prophylactic surgery at >4.5-5.0 cm.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','easy','flashcard','What are the clinical consequences of a large abdominal aortic aneurysm before rupture?','["A large AAA (>5 cm) can cause symptoms from: (1) Pulsatile abdominal mass (felt as a pulsating epigastric mass, transmitted pulsations from the aorta); (2) Abdominal or back pain (expansion or contained leak; if sudden severe pain = impending rupture); (3) Distal embolisation: laminated mural thrombus fragments → cholesterol emboli to lower extremities (''blue toe syndrome'', livedo reticularis, digital gangrene with normal distal pulses); (4) Compression of adjacent structures: duodenal obstruction (early satiety, vomiting), ureteral compression (hydronephrosis, hydroureter — especially inflammatory AAA), venous compression (IVC → lower limb oedema); (5) Aortoenteric or aortocaval fistula (rare): AAA erodes into duodenum (catastrophic GI bleed) or IVC (high-output HF, pulsatile venous flow)."]','A','Most AAAs are asymptomatic until rupture — found incidentally on imaging. Surveillance ultrasound is recommended for men 65-75 who have ever smoked (US Preventive Services Task Force). The ''pulsatile abdominal mass'' = classic physical examination sign of large AAA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','medium','flashcard','What is the role of matrix metalloproteinases in AAA pathogenesis?','["MMP-mediated matrix destruction is central to AAA formation: Key MMPs in AAA: MMP-9 (gelatinase B) — degrades denatured collagen and gelatin; major source = macrophages and neutrophils; elevated in AAA tissue and plasma. MMP-12 (macrophage metalloelastase) — degrades elastin; major source = macrophages. MMP-2 (gelatinase A) — degrades collagen IV and elastin; from SMCs and fibroblasts. Regulatory context: MMPs are balanced by TIMPs (tissue inhibitors of metalloproteinases — TIMP-1, TIMP-2). In AAA: MMP activity > TIMP activity → net matrix breakdown. Therapeutic implications: Doxycycline (MMP inhibitor), statins, and beta-blockers all reduce MMP activity in experimental models and may slow AAA expansion (but no drug is yet proven to replace surgical repair in large AAAs)."]','A','MMP-9 = the star of AAA pathogenesis. Macrophages = the main producers. The imbalance MMP > TIMP = aortic wall weakness. This explains why smoking (causes macrophage influx) is such a major AAA risk factor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the classification of vasculitis by vessel size and give examples of each?','["Large vessel vasculitis (aorta and major branches): Giant cell (temporal) arteritis, Takayasu arteritis. Medium vessel vasculitis (main visceral arteries and branches): Polyarteritis nodosa, Kawasaki disease. Small vessel vasculitis (arterioles, capillaries, venules): ANCA-associated: Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA/Wegener), Microscopic polyangiitis, Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss). Immune complex-associated: IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein), cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis, anti-GBM disease. Buerger disease (medium-small): Thromboangiitis obliterans (small and medium arteries of extremities in young smokers). Behcet disease: Multi-sized vessel vasculitis with orogenital ulcers and uveitis. Note: Many vasculitides can affect overlapping vessel sizes."]','A','Key for exams: Large = GCA, Takayasu. Medium = PAN, Kawasaki. Small = ANCA (GPA, MPA, EGPA) + immune complex (IgA vasculitis). Remember: PAN does NOT affect capillaries or venules (only arteries) — this distinguishes it from MPA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the pathological features of giant cell (temporal) arteritis?','["GCA is a granulomatous vasculitis affecting large and medium-sized arteries, predominantly cranial branches. Pathological features on biopsy: (1) Granulomatous inflammation within the vessel wall (panarteritis — all layers affected): epithelioid histiocytes, multinucleate giant cells (clustered around the fragmented internal elastic lamina), T lymphocytes, macrophages. (2) Fragmentation and destruction of the internal elastic lamina (giant cells engulf fragments — the elastic lamina appears to be the primary target). (3) Intimal hyperplasia narrowing the lumen. (4) Adventitial and periadventitial inflammation. Note: Granulomas are present in ~50-70% of biopsies; their absence does NOT exclude GCA (disease is focal with skip lesions). Required biopsy length: at least 1 cm (skip lesions). Associated condition: polymyalgia rheumatica (PMR) occurs in ~50% of GCA patients."]','A','GCA biopsy: giant cells at the internal elastic lamina (which is fragmented). Skip lesions → need long biopsy (≥1 cm). PMR + GCA = 50% overlap. Age >50 is essentially a criterion — GCA is very rare under 50. ESR typically >50 mm/hr, often >100.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the ANCA subtypes and which vasculitides are associated with each?','["ANCA (anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies) are autoantibodies against components of neutrophil granules: c-ANCA (cytoplasmic pattern on immunofluorescence): Target antigen = Proteinase 3 (PR3). Associated with: Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA/Wegener) — ~90% sensitivity and specificity for active GPA. p-ANCA (perinuclear pattern): Target antigen = Myeloperoxidase (MPO). Associated with: Microscopic polyangiitis (MPA), Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA/Churg-Strauss), some cases of pauci-immune glomerulonephritis, drug-induced vasculitis (propylthiouracil → MPO-ANCA). Note: Some cases of PAN are ANCA-positive (controversial); most PAN is ANCA-negative. Atypical ANCA: Can be seen in IBD, autoimmune hepatitis, primary sclerosing cholangitis (but not associated with systemic vasculitis)."]','A','c-ANCA/PR3 = Wegener (GPA). p-ANCA/MPO = MPA + EGPA. Memory: ''c for Wegener (c-ANCA), p for MPA (p-ANCA).'' ANCA testing guides diagnosis AND treatment monitoring (rising titer = relapse risk).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the pathogenesis of Kawasaki disease and what are the diagnostic criteria?','["Kawasaki disease (mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome): Medium-vessel vasculitis predominantly affecting children (<5 years; peak 18-24 months). Pathogenesis: Unknown trigger (possibly viral or superantigen); abnormal immune activation with T-cell and macrophage infiltration of medium vessel walls (similar to PAN histologically, but usually less fibrinoid necrosis); IL-1β, TNF-α, and IL-6 mediate inflammation. The vasculitis preferentially affects coronary arteries. Diagnostic criteria: Fever ≥5 days + ≥4 of the following ''CREAM'' criteria: (1) Conjunctivitis (non-purulent, bilateral); (2) Rash (polymorphous exanthem); (3) Erythema/desquamation of hands and feet; (4) Adenopathy (cervical lymph nodes, usually unilateral, >1.5 cm); (5) Mucous membrane changes (strawberry tongue, cracked/erythematous lips, oropharyngeal erythema). Treatment: IVIG (2 g/kg single dose) + aspirin. IVIG reduces coronary aneurysm risk from 20% to <5%."]','A','CREAM = Conjunctivitis, Rash, Erythema (hands/feet), Adenopathy, Mucous membrane changes. Kawasaki = most common cause of acquired heart disease in children in developed countries. If untreated: giant coronary artery aneurysms → rupture, thrombosis, MI, SCD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','flashcard','What distinguishes polyarteritis nodosa from microscopic polyangiitis?','["Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN): Necrotising vasculitis of MEDIUM-SIZED muscular arteries. Does NOT affect capillaries, venules, or arterioles. Renal involvement = renal artery vasculitis (ischaemic renal damage) but NOT glomerulonephritis (glomeruli are capillaries). ANCA negative. Associated with hepatitis B (30%). Multiple microaneurysms on angiography (from nodular weakening of arterial walls = ''nodosa''). Segmental, with skip areas. Microscopic polyangiitis (MPA): Necrotising vasculitis of SMALL vessels (capillaries, arterioles, venules). Renal involvement = pauci-immune necrotising glomerulonephritis (very common). Pulmonary capillaritis (haemoptysis). p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA) positive in 70%. No granulomas. Key distinction: PAN = no glomerulonephritis, no ANCA (usually), medium arteries. MPA = glomerulonephritis + pulmonary capillaritis + p-ANCA, small vessels."]','A','PAN vs MPA: PAN affects arteries (no capillaries → no GN). MPA affects capillaries (GN + pulmonary haemorrhage). PAN = hepatitis B. MPA = p-ANCA. PAN on angiography = ''string of pearls'' microaneurysms. MPA = pauci-immune GN on biopsy (minimal immune deposits by immunofluorescence).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','hard','flashcard','What are the pathological features of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) in the lungs and kidneys?','["Lungs: Bilateral nodular cavitating lesions (can be large, >3 cm); geographic necrotising granulomas that span between vessels with central necrosis and surrounding palisading histiocytes and giant cells; alveolar haemorrhage from necrotising capillaritis. Upper airway: Necrotising granulomas of sinuses, nasal septum, palate → saddle-nose deformity from septal destruction. Kidneys: Focal or diffuse pauci-immune necrotising glomerulonephritis: (a) Focal segmental necrosis in early disease (focal and segmental necrotising GN); (b) Crescentic GN with fibrous crescents in severe disease → rapidly progressive GN (RPGN). Key: ''Pauci-immune'' = very little or no immunoglobulin/complement deposited by immunofluorescence (unlike immune complex GN). PR3-ANCA is pathogenic. Treatment: Induction = cyclophosphamide or rituximab + high-dose steroids. Maintenance = rituximab or azathioprine."]','A','GPA triad: (1) Upper airway necrotising granulomas (saddle-nose), (2) Pulmonary cavitating nodules, (3) Pauci-immune crescentic GN. c-ANCA/PR3 = diagnostic. ''Pauci-immune'' = the opposite of lupus nephritis (which is complement-rich).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','hard','flashcard','What is Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease) and what is unique about its pathology?','["TAO (Buerger disease): Segmental, thrombosing, inflammatory disease of small and medium arteries and veins of the distal extremities. Almost exclusively in young male heavy smokers (typically <40 years; women increasingly affected with increasing female smoking). The only vasculitis with MANDATORY obligate tobacco exposure. Pathological features UNIQUE to TAO: (1) The inflammatory thrombus within the vessel lumen is the primary lesion — it is highly cellular with polymorphonuclear cells and microabscesses within the thrombus; (2) Multinucleate giant cells may be present WITHIN the thrombus (unusual — usually only in granulomatous vasculitis); (3) The VESSEL WALL is relatively PRESERVED — the internal elastic lamina remains INTACT (crucial distinguishing feature from other vasculitides where the elastic lamina is destroyed); (4) Segmental lesions with skip areas. Clinical: digital ischaemia, Raynaud phenomenon, superficial thrombophlebitis, ''rest pain''. Only effective treatment = complete smoking cessation."]','A','Buerger''s pathological hallmark: inflammatory thrombus WITH INTACT vessel wall elastic lamina. This distinguishes it from PAN (fibrinoid necrosis, intact IEL is NOT a feature). The IEL is preserved in Buerger''s disease. Young male smoker + digital ischaemia + SVT = Buerger disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','easy','flashcard','What is Raynaud phenomenon and how is it classified?','["Raynaud phenomenon: Episodic, reversible vasospasm of small arteries and arterioles of the extremities (fingers, toes, less commonly nose, ears) triggered by cold or emotional stress. Three phases: White (pallor — vasospasm, ischaemia), Blue (cyanosis — deoxygenation), Red (erythema — reactive hyperaemia). Primary Raynaud phenomenon (Raynaud disease): No underlying disease; affects young women; benign; no structural vessel damage. Secondary Raynaud phenomenon: Underlying disease causes structural vascular changes; more severe, leads to digital ulcers/infarcts/gangrene. Causes of secondary: Systemic sclerosis/scleroderma (most common — nearly all scleroderma patients have Raynaud); SLE; MCTD; Sjögren; CREST syndrome; occupational (vibration — ''vibration white finger''); drugs (beta-blockers, ergotamine, cisplatin). Pathogenesis: Exaggerated sympathetic vasoconstriction + reduced NO/PGI2 production + increased endothelin."]','A','Primary Raynaud = benign, young women, no ulcers. Secondary Raynaud = serious, associated with autoimmune disease (especially scleroderma), can cause tissue loss. The ''tricolour'' = White → Blue → Red = classic description.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','medium','flashcard','How does Takayasu arteritis differ from giant cell arteritis in terms of patient demographics, distribution, and pathology?','["Takayasu arteritis vs Giant Cell Arteritis: Takayasu: Age: Young women, typically <40 years (mean 25 years); GCA: Older adults >50 years (mean 70 years). Takayasu: Ethnicity: Originally described in Asian women but now global; GCA: Northern European/Caucasian predilection. Distribution: Takayasu classically affects the AORTIC ARCH and major branches (great vessels — subclavian, carotid, vertebral); also involves the entire aorta and pulmonary arteries in some cases. GCA affects cranial branches of the aortic arch (temporal, ophthalmic, posterior ciliary, vertebral). Histology: Both show granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate. Clinical hallmarks: Takayasu = ''pulseless disease'' (upper limb claudication, arm blood pressure discrepancy, reduced/absent pulses); GCA = headache, jaw claudication, visual loss, scalp tenderness. Both treated with corticosteroids."]','A','Age is the key discriminator: Takayasu = young women (called the ''young woman''s disease'' of the aorta). GCA = elderly (>50, usually >60). Both are granulomatous large-vessel vasculitides. ESR and CRP elevated in both; biopsy/imaging (FDG-PET) confirms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','hard','flashcard','What is leukocytoclastic vasculitis (LCV) and what conditions cause it?','["Leukocytoclastic vasculitis (LCV) = hypersensitivity vasculitis = small vessel vasculitis typically affecting skin (dermis/superficial venules). Pathological features: (1) Transmural neutrophilic infiltration of small vessel walls; (2) Leukocytoclasis = fragmentation of neutrophil nuclei leaving ''nuclear dust'' (pyknotic fragments) — pathognomonic; (3) Fibrinoid necrosis; (4) Red cell extravasation → palpable purpura. Mechanism: Type III hypersensitivity (immune complex deposition in small vessels → complement activation → neutrophil recruitment → oxidative burst and enzyme release). Causes: Drugs (most common — penicillins, sulfonamides, phenytoin); infections (hepatitis B, C, streptococcal); IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura); cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis; CTD (SLE, RA); idiopathic. Clinical: Palpable purpura (palpable because it is inflammatory — unlike thrombocytopenic purpura which is flat/non-palpable), urticaria, arthralgias. Resolution on withdrawal of offending agent (if drug-induced)."]','A','Leukocytoclasis = ''nuclear dust'' from neutrophil fragmentation in vessel walls = pathognomonic. Palpable purpura = LCV until proven otherwise. Most common cause = drug reaction. IgA vasculitis (HSP) = IgA immune complexes + LCV in children after upper respiratory infection (IgA, palpable purpura, arthritis, nephritis, abdominal pain = PAIN = Purpura, Arthritis, Intestinal, Nephritis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Atherosclerosis & Arterial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is Mönckeberg medial calcific sclerosis and why is it clinically important?','["Mönckeberg medial calcific sclerosis (medial calcinosis): Calcification of the MEDIA (not intima) of muscular arteries, typically occurring in the elderly and in diabetics. Features: (1) Calcium deposits form in the smooth muscle cells of the media, producing a ''pipestem'' or ''tram-track'' pattern on X-ray; (2) Does NOT involve the intima; (3) Does NOT narrow the lumen → does NOT cause ischaemia by itself; (4) Not associated with atherosclerosis pathogenically (though both may coexist in elderly/diabetics). Clinical importance: (1) May give falsely elevated ankle-brachial index (ABI) because calcified arteries are non-compressible — falsely high systolic pressure → falsely elevated ABI → may miss PAD; (2) May indicate vascular disease in diabetics; (3) On imaging, calcified ''eggshell'' vessels distinguish it from atherosclerotic intimal calcification."]','A','Mönckeberg = medial calcification = pipestem arteries on X-ray = no ischaemia = falsely high ABI. This is distinct from atherosclerotic calcification (intimal, associated with plaque). Important in diabetics who often have both.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Ischemic Heart Disease (Angina, MI, Sudden Cardiac Death)','medium','flashcard','What is the microvascular basis of ischaemia in patients with cardiac syndrome X?','["Cardiac syndrome X (microvascular angina): Angina pectoris + positive exercise test + NORMAL epicardial coronary arteries on angiography. The ischaemia arises from coronary microvascular dysfunction (CMD): (1) Impaired microvascular vasodilation: endothelial dysfunction in small coronary arteries/arterioles → reduced nitric oxide → blunted hyperaemic response to adenosine/exercise; (2) Abnormal microvascular resistance: smooth muscle hyperreactivity in small vessels → microvascular spasm; (3) Reduced coronary flow reserve: inability to increase flow appropriately with demand. Pathological correlate: Microvascular disease is common in hypertensive LVH (compression of intramural vessels, reduced capillary density), diabetes (microvascular basement membrane thickening), and women (smaller coronary artery calibre). Diagnosis: invasive coronary reactivity testing with intracoronary acetylcholine/adenosine."]','A','Cardiac syndrome X = angina with normal epicardial arteries = microvascular disease. More common in women, diabetics, hypertensives. Troponin may still rise (microvascular ischaemia). Treatment: nitrates, beta-blockers, ranolazine, statins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Heart Failure (systolic vs diastolic, compensatory mechanisms)','medium','flashcard','What are the indications for cardiac biomarkers (BNP/NT-proBNP) in heart failure?','["BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide) and NT-proBNP (N-terminal pro-BNP) are secreted by ventricular cardiomyocytes in response to wall stretch (volume and pressure overload). Uses: (1) Diagnosis: Differentiates cardiac dyspnoea from pulmonary dyspnoea in the emergency setting (BNP >100 pg/mL or NT-proBNP >300 pg/mL suggests HF as cause of acute dyspnoea); (2) Prognosis: Higher levels correlate with worse outcomes; (3) Monitoring treatment response: BNP-guided therapy aims to reduce BNP toward normal (target BNP <35 pg/mL, NT-proBNP <125 pg/mL); (4) Risk stratification in asymptomatic patients with structural heart disease. False positives (elevated BNP without HF): renal failure, PE, severe pulmonary hypertension, sepsis, AF. False negatives (normal BNP in HF): obesity (dilutional effect), early flash pulmonary oedema, HFpEF with mild elevation."]','A','BNP = the ''wall stress hormone.'' Elevated = heart is under strain. BNP >500 = likely HF; 100-500 = grey zone; <100 = unlikely HF. NT-proBNP uses different cut-offs and rises higher (longer half-life). Obesity lowers BNP (adipose tissue degrades it).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Hypertensive Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the ocular manifestations of hypertension (hypertensive retinopathy) and how are they graded?','["Hypertensive retinopathy reflects microvascular changes from chronic hypertension. Keith-Wagener-Barker classification: Grade I: Mild arterial narrowing and arteriolar attenuation (''silver wiring'' from arteriosclerosis); arteriovenous nipping at crossing points. Grade II: More pronounced arteriolar narrowing (''copper wiring''); definite AV nicking/nipping; moderate. Grade III: Haemorrhages (flame-shaped from superficial nerve fibre layer, or dot/blot from deeper layers), cotton-wool spots (microinfarcts of nerve fibre layer from arteriolar occlusion), hard exudates (lipid deposits from leaking vessels). Grade IV: All of Grade III + PAPILLOEDEMA (disc swelling from raised intracranial pressure in malignant hypertension). Grades III-IV = malignant hypertension — vision-threatening and indicate immediate blood pressure reduction."]','A','Grade IV hypertensive retinopathy = papilloedema = malignant hypertension = emergency. Cotton-wool spots = microinfarcts (''soft exudates''). Hard exudates = lipid leakage from damaged vessels forming a ''macular star'' pattern around the fovea in chronic hypertension.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Valvular Heart Disease (stenosis vs regurgitation)','hard','flashcard','What is infective endocarditis and what are the Duke criteria for diagnosis?','["Infective endocarditis (IE): Microbial infection of the endocardium, typically the cardiac valves. Modified Duke Criteria — 2 major OR 1 major + 3 minor OR 5 minor = definite IE: Major criteria: (1) Positive blood cultures: typical organisms (S. viridans, S. bovis, HACEK, S. aureus, or Enterococcus from community) in 2 separate cultures; or persistently positive cultures ≥12 hrs apart; or single positive for Coxiella burnetii; (2) Endocardial involvement: positive echocardiography (vegetation, abscess, new dehiscence of prosthetic valve) OR new valvular regurgitation. Minor criteria: (1) Predisposing cardiac condition or IV drug use; (2) Fever ≥38°C; (3) Vascular phenomena: arterial emboli, septic pulmonary infarcts, Janeway lesions, mycotic aneurysms, conjunctival haemorrhages; (4) Immunological phenomena: Osler nodes, Roth spots, glomerulonephritis, RF positive; (5) Microbiological evidence not meeting major criteria."]','A','Duke criteria is the standard for IE diagnosis. Janeway lesions = painless (emboli to palm/sole). Osler nodes = PAINFUL (immune complex deposition on finger/toe pads). Roth spots = retinal haemorrhages with pale centres.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiomyopathies (dilated, hypertrophic, restrictive)','medium','flashcard','What is stress (Takotsubo) cardiomyopathy and what is its pathogenesis?','["Takotsubo cardiomyopathy (stress cardiomyopathy, ''broken heart syndrome,'' apical ballooning syndrome): Transient, reversible LV dysfunction characterised by apical ballooning and hyperdynamic basal LV function without epicardial coronary artery occlusion. Name: ''Takotsubo'' = Japanese octopus trap — the ballooned LV apex resembles this shape on contrast ventriculography. Demographics: Predominantly post-menopausal women (90%); triggered by severe emotional or physical stress. Pathogenesis: Catecholamine surge (massive sympathetic activation from stress): (1) Coronary microvascular spasm → transient ischaemia; (2) Direct catecholamine toxicity to cardiomyocytes (catecholamine-mediated myocardial injury); (3) Apical cardiomyocytes are more sensitive to catecholamines (higher adrenergic receptor density at apex). Troponin mildly elevated. Echo: apical wall motion abnormalities + hyperdynamic base (EF may be <20%). Resolution: Full LV function recovery in 4-8 weeks in most cases."]','A','Takotsubo = ''emotional'' or ''physical stress'' + post-menopausal woman + apical ballooning + normal epicardial arteries + full recovery. Mimics STEMI but PCI shows no culprit lesion. Catecholamines = the mediator.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the pathogenesis and management of recurrent pericarditis?','["Recurrent pericarditis: Pericarditis that recurs after a symptom-free interval ≥4-6 weeks after the initial episode. Occurs in 15-30% of patients after a first episode. Pathogenesis: Predominantly autoimmune in most cases (antibodies against pericardial antigens, complement activation, cytokine-mediated inflammation). Some cases may represent inadequate initial treatment, unrecognised viral persistence, or a true autoimmune response (sometimes associated with anti-dsDNA, ANA, anti-SS-A). Management: (1) Colchicine (anti-inflammatory, inhibits interleukin-1β processing and inflammasome activation) — reduces recurrence rate by ~50%; should be continued for 6 months; (2) NSAIDs (ibuprofen, aspirin); (3) Corticosteroids (risk of dependence and rebound; used sparingly); (4) Azathioprine or IVIG for refractory cases; (5) Anti-IL-1 therapy (anakinra, rilonacept) — highly effective for refractory recurrent pericarditis (now FDA approved for this indication)."]','A','Colchicine = cornerstone of recurrent pericarditis prevention (halves recurrence rate). Anti-IL-1 therapy (anakinra, rilonacept) = breakthrough for refractory cases. Corticosteroids = avoid if possible (rebound effect increases recurrence).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Congenital Heart Disease (ASD, VSD, Tetralogy, TGA, PDA)','medium','flashcard','What is truncus arteriosus and how does it differ from Tetralogy of Fallot?','["Truncus arteriosus: A single great artery (truncus) arises from the heart, overriding a large VSD, and gives rise to systemic, pulmonary, and coronary circulations. Embryological basis: Failure of the aortopulmonary septum to form (normally divides the truncus into aorta and pulmonary artery). Result: Single common great vessel receiving blood from both ventricles; obligate mixing → cyanosis (if pulmonary vascular resistance is low, there is a lot of pulmonary blood flow → ''pink'' truncus with pulmonary overcirculation; if PVR is high → cyanosis). Strong association: 22q11 (DiGeorge) deletion. Comparison with Tetralogy of Fallot: TOF = 4 separate anomalies, 2 vessels present (aorta and PA both exist, just RVOTO reduces pulmonary flow). Truncus = only 1 vessel arising from heart (PA doesn''t form separately). TOF = RVOTO → reduced pulmonary flow. Truncus = pulmonary overcirculation initially."]','A','Truncus = the aortopulmonary septum never formed → 1 vessel. TOF = septum formed but shifted anteriorly → RVOTO + VSD + overriding aorta + RVH. Both involve a VSD and overriding vessel, but in truncus there is only ONE vessel total.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Cardiac Tumours & Thrombi','medium','flashcard','What are the pathological features that distinguish primary benign cardiac tumours from each other?','["Key distinguishing features: Myxoma: Atria (90% left); fossa ovalis attachment; gelatinous polypoid mass; myxoid stroma with ''lepidic'' cells; fragments can embolise; may cause constitutional symptoms (IL-6). Papillary fibroelastoma: Valve surfaces (aortic most common); small (<1 cm); papillary fronds with elastic fibre core; sea-anemone appearance; may embolise. Lipoma: Any location (including interatrial septum — ''lipomatous hypertrophy of interatrial septum''); encapsulated fat; incidental. Rhabdomyoma: Multiple, ventricular; children; tuberous sclerosis; spider cells; regresses spontaneously. Fibroma: Ventricular myocardium (LV/septum); children; firm white mass; fibroblasts and collagen; does NOT regress; may contain calcification; associated with Gorlin syndrome (PTCH mutation). Haemangioma: Can occur anywhere in heart; thin-walled blood-filled channels."]','A','Quick distinguisher table: Myxoma = LA fossa ovalis gelatinous. Fibroelastoma = valve surface papillary fronds elastic fibres. Lipoma = fat anywhere. Rhabdomyoma = child, TS, spider cells, regresses. Fibroma = child, LV/septum, solid, doesn''t regress.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Aortic Diseases (aneurysm, dissection)','hard','flashcard','What is the role of TGF-β signalling in Marfan syndrome aortopathy?','["In Marfan syndrome, FBN1 (fibrillin-1) mutations disrupt the extracellular microfibrillar scaffold. Two mechanisms contribute to aortopathy: (1) Structural defect: Fibrillin-1 is essential for elastic fibre assembly → loss of structural integrity of the aortic wall → weakened media. (2) Dysregulated TGF-β signalling: Fibrillin-1 normally sequesters TGF-β in the extracellular matrix. Without functional fibrillin-1, TGF-β is released in excess → increased TGF-β signalling → aortic SMC dysfunction, increased MMP production, and pathological extracellular matrix remodelling → progressive aortic root dilation. Therapeutic implications: Losartan (AT1R blocker = TGF-β antagonist) reduces TGF-β signalling in the aortic wall → slows aortic root dilation in Marfan patients (clinical trial data supportive). Beta-blockers reduce haemodynamic stress (dP/dt). Combined losartan + atenolol used in Marfan management."]','A','Fibrillin-1 = structural scaffolding + TGF-β reservoir. Loss of fibrillin = weaker wall AND too much TGF-β → progressive aortopathy. Losartan = the TGF-β antagonist that slows Marfan aortic root dilation. This is a good example of pathogenesis directly guiding therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Pathology','Vasculitis & Small Vessel Disease','hard','flashcard','What is the role of the inflammasome in vasculitis pathogenesis, and what therapeutic target does this suggest?','["The NLRP3 inflammasome is a cytoplasmic multiprotein complex that, when activated, processes pro-IL-1β to mature IL-1β and pro-IL-18 to IL-18, triggering pyroptosis (inflammatory cell death) and a potent inflammatory cascade. In vasculitis: IL-1β is a key mediator of vascular inflammation and systemic inflammatory features (fever, elevated CRP, leukocytosis). Diseases where inflammasome/IL-1β plays a role: (1) GCA: IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α from macrophages in vessel walls drive granulomatous inflammation; (2) Kawasaki disease: NLRP3 inflammasome activation → IL-1β surges; (3) Recurrent pericarditis (FMF-like autoinflammatory mechanism): IL-1β-mediated. Therapeutic targets: (1) Colchicine: inhibits NLRP3 inflammasome assembly (tubulin disruption prevents caspase-1 activation); (2) Anakinra (IL-1Ra), canakinumab (anti-IL-1β), rilonacept (IL-1 trap): direct IL-1 blockade, effective in recurrent pericarditis and Kawasaki."]','A','NLRP3 inflammasome → caspase-1 → IL-1β = the inflammatory gasoline of vasculitis and pericarditis. Colchicine blocks the inflammasome. Anakinra/canakinumab block IL-1β directly. This pathway is now a validated therapeutic target.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
;
